var e=[{id:`monet-impression-sunrise`,kind:`painting`,title:`Impression, Sunrise`,artist:`Claude Monet`,date:`1872`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris`,caption:`The harbour of Le Havre at dawn, seen through mist, in the painting whose title gave Impressionism its name.`,line:{text:`Wallpaper in its embryonic state is more complete than this water-view!`,by:`Louis Leroy`,context:`in Le Charivari, 25 April 1874, of this picture. He meant it as mockery; the painters took the name.`,source:`Louis Leroy, “L’exposition des impressionnistes”, Le Charivari, 25 April 1874, pp. 79–80, translated by Terence Maloon; Art Gallery of New South Wales, Monet and the Impressionists education kit (2008), note 8`,url:`https://archive.artgallery.nsw.gov.au/__data/page/12200/Monet_ed_kit.pdf`},about:`Monet painted the harbour of Le Havre at dawn from the window of his hotel room, in a few hours, around November 1872. The rowboats and the orange sun with its reflections were added last. Asked for a title for the catalogue of the 1874 group exhibition, he felt the canvas could not pass for a view of Le Havre and called it Impression. The show, in the photographer Nadar’s former studios, drew 3,500 visitors; the official Salon that year drew 450,000.`,learnMore:{label:`Musée Marmottan Monet`,url:`https://www.marmottan.fr/en/notice/4014/`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:65,heightCm:50},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monet_-_Impression,_Sunrise.jpg`,ratio:1.289},{id:`manet-dejeuner-sur-lherbe`,kind:`painting`,title:`Luncheon on the Grass`,artist:`Édouard Manet`,date:`1863`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Musée d'Orsay, Paris`,caption:`A nude woman sits at a picnic in a wood with two men in modern dress, the picture at the centre of the scandal of 1863.`,line:{text:`The Salon jury rejected it in 1863. Manet showed it as Le Bain at the Salon des Refusés, where it drew laughter and scandal.`,context:`The Salon des Refusés was set up that year by Napoleon III for rejected works.`,source:`Musée d’Orsay, notice for Le Déjeuner sur l’herbe, RF 1668`,url:`https://www.musee-orsay.fr/en/artworks/le-dejeuner-sur-lherbe-904`},about:`A nude woman sits at a picnic in a wood with two men in modern dress. Manet took the subject from a Titian in the Louvre, then thought to be by Giorgione, and the pose of the central group from an engraving after Raphael. No myth or allegory explained the nude, so the public found the scene obscene. The handling shocked too: abrupt contrasts in place of soft shading, and a wood painted as a flat backdrop.`,learnMore:{label:`Musée d'Orsay`,url:`https://www.musee-orsay.fr/en/artworks/le-dejeuner-sur-lherbe-904`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:265,heightCm:207},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Musée d'Orsay, Paris, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edouard_Manet_-_Luncheon_on_the_Grass_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:1.2871},{id:`morisot-the-cradle`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Cradle`,artist:`Berthe Morisot`,date:`1872`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Musée d'Orsay, Paris`,caption:`Morisot's sister Edma watches over her sleeping daughter, Blanche, in her cradle.`,line:{text:`Morisot could not sell it. It stayed with her sister’s family until the Louvre bought it in 1930.`,context:`She had shown it at the first Impressionist exhibition in 1874.`,source:`Musée d’Orsay, notice for Le Berceau, RF 2849`,url:`https://www.musee-orsay.fr/en/artworks/le-berceau-1132`},about:`Morisot’s sister Edma watches over her sleeping daughter Blanche. It is the first image of motherhood in Morisot’s work, later one of her favourite subjects. Edma’s hand draws the net curtain of the cradle between the viewer and the baby. Morisot was the first woman to exhibit with the group in 1874, and the picture was scarcely noticed, though some critics remarked on its grace.`,learnMore:{label:`Musée d'Orsay`,url:`https://www.musee-orsay.fr/en/artworks/le-berceau-1132`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:46.5,heightCm:56},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Musée d'Orsay, Paris, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berthe_Morisot_-_The_Cradle_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:.8209},{id:`monet-woman-with-a-parasol`,kind:`painting`,title:`Woman with a Parasol – Madame Monet and Her Son`,artist:`Claude Monet`,date:`1875`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`National Gallery of Art, Washington`,caption:`Camille Monet and her son pause on a grassy rise under a bright, breezy sky, seen from below.`,line:{text:`Monet painted it outdoors, probably in a single session of several hours.`,context:`It is a family walk interrupted for a moment, not a formal portrait.`,source:`National Gallery of Art, collection text for Woman with a Parasol – Madame Monet and Her Son, 1983.1.29`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/61379-woman-parasol-madame-monet-and-her-son`},about:`Camille Monet and her son Jean pause on a grassy rise, seen from below against a bright, breezy sky. Sunlight from behind whitens the top of the parasol and the cloth at her back, while light reflected from the wildflowers touches her front with yellow. Monet painted his sitters’ features as freely as their surroundings. At the second Impressionist exhibition in 1876 the picture was praised for its spontaneity.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery of Art`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/61379-woman-parasol-madame-monet-and-her-son`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:81,heightCm:100},licence:`cc0`,credit:`National Gallery of Art, Washington, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nga.gov/collection/art-object-page.61379.html`,ratio:.8067},{id:`monet-beach-at-sainte-adresse`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Beach at Sainte-Adresse`,artist:`Claude Monet`,date:`1867`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Art Institute of Chicago`,caption:`Fishing boats lie on the shore at Sainte-Adresse, near Le Havre, while a fashionably dressed couple looks out to sea.`,line:{text:`Monet first painted more tourists and yachts, then painted over them to put the fishermen at the centre.`,context:`He began the picture outdoors in the summer of 1867 and reworked it in the studio.`,source:`Art Institute of Chicago, collection entry for The Beach at Sainte-Adresse, 1933.439`,url:`https://www.artic.edu/artworks/14598/the-beach-at-sainte-adresse`},about:`Monet spent the summer of 1867 with his aunt at Sainte-Adresse, near Le Havre, where he grew up. The railway was turning the fishing village into a resort, and the couple on the shore, one with a telescope, are tourists picked out in a few strokes of red and yellow. Monet sold the picture to the dealer Durand-Ruel in 1873 for 400 francs and showed it at the second Impressionist exhibition in 1876. In 1920 it changed hands for 40,000 francs.`,learnMore:{label:`Art Institute of Chicago`,url:`https://www.artic.edu/artworks/14598/the-beach-at-sainte-adresse`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:102.5,heightCm:75.8},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Art Institute of Chicago, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_Monet_-_The_Beach_at_Sainte-Adresse_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:1.3531},{id:`caillebotte-paris-street-rainy-day`,kind:`painting`,title:`Paris Street; Rainy Day`,artist:`Gustave Caillebotte`,date:`1877`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Art Institute of Chicago`,caption:`People with umbrellas cross a wide, wet intersection in the newly rebuilt Paris of the 1870s.`,line:{text:`There are no painted raindrops. The rain is in the umbrellas and the wet paving.`,context:`The umbrellas are of a newly available retractable kind.`,source:`Art Institute of Chicago, collection entry for Paris Street; Rainy Day, 1964.336`,url:`https://www.artic.edu/artworks/20684/paris-street-rainy-day`},about:`People with umbrellas cross a wide intersection in the newly rebuilt Paris of the 1870s, a short walk from Caillebotte’s home; at 212 by 276 cm it is his largest painting. The sharp cropping may owe something to photography, and the couple in front seem about to walk into the viewer. It was number 1 in the catalogue of the third Impressionist exhibition in 1877, which Caillebotte did much to organise. The Art Institute bought it in 1964.`,learnMore:{label:`Art Institute of Chicago`,url:`https://www.artic.edu/artworks/20684/paris-street-rainy-day`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:276.2,heightCm:212.2},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Art Institute of Chicago, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustave_Caillebotte_-_Paris_Street;_Rainy_Day_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:1.32},{id:`renoir-luncheon-of-the-boating-party`,kind:`painting`,title:`Luncheon of the Boating Party`,artist:`Pierre-Auguste Renoir`,date:`1880–81`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Phillips Collection, Washington`,caption:`Renoir's friends linger after lunch on the balcony of a restaurant on the Seine, under a striped awning.`,line:{text:`The awning was a late addition. Under its thin paint, sky and the whole bridge once filled the top of the picture.`,context:`Infrared and surface study by the Phillips Collection’s conservators.`,source:`The Phillips Collection, “Beneath the Surface of Luncheon of the Boating Party (Part 2)”, 6 December 2017`,url:`https://www.phillipscollection.org/blog/2017-12-06-beneath-surface-luncheon-boating-party-part-2`},about:`Renoir’s friends linger after lunch on the balcony of a restaurant on the Seine. Most of the sitters have been identified: the painter Gustave Caillebotte sits backwards on his chair, and Aline Charigot, Renoir’s future wife, plays with her terrier. Infrared images show that the man in the top hat first looked out over the balcony before Renoir turned him into conversation, and that a wine glass was painted out of the table. Duncan Phillips bought the picture in 1923.`,learnMore:{label:`The Phillips Collection`,url:`https://www.phillipscollection.org/collection/luncheon-boating-party`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:175.6,heightCm:130.2},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`The Phillips Collection, Washington, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre-Auguste_Renoir_-_Luncheon_of_the_Boating_Party_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:1.3502},{id:`seurat-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte`,kind:`painting`,title:`A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884`,artist:`Georges Seurat`,date:`1884–86, border added 1888–89`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Art Institute of Chicago`,caption:`Parisians of every age and class spend a Sunday on an island park in the Seine, painted in small dots and dashes of colour.`,line:{text:`I would have noted that myself, Pissarro, except that the painting is so big!`,by:`Edgar Degas`,context:`to Camille Pissarro, who had told him Seurat’s picture was very interesting, as the 1886 exhibition was being arranged. Pissarro reported it to his son Lucien.`,source:`Camille Pissarro, Letters to His Son Lucien, ed. John Rewald, trans. Lionel Abel (New York: Pantheon Books, 1943), letter from Paris, March 1886, p. 73`,url:`https://archive.org/stream/lettersto00piss/lettersto00piss_djvu.txt`},about:`Parisians of every age and class spend a Sunday on an island park in the Seine, painted in small touches of colour that Seurat believed would mix in the viewer’s eye. He came back to the canvas two years after starting it, strengthening silhouettes and adding figures such as the monkey on a leash. It was first shown at the eighth and last Impressionist exhibition in 1886, where the critic Félix Fénéon coined the term Neo-Impressionism. Seurat’s brother sold it in 1900 for 800 francs; it was given to the Art Institute in 1926.`,learnMore:{label:`Art Institute of Chicago`,url:`https://www.artic.edu/artworks/27992/a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:308.1,heightCm:207.5},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Art Institute of Chicago, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georges_Seurat_-_A_Sunday_on_La_Grande_Jatte_--_1884_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:1.5023},{id:`monet-water-lilies-1906`,kind:`painting`,title:`Water Lilies`,artist:`Claude Monet`,date:`1906`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Art Institute of Chicago`,caption:`Clusters of water lilies float on Monet's garden pond at Giverny, whose surface reflects trees and clouds.`,line:{text:`Monet called these pictures an obsession. He painted the pond at Giverny around 250 times over twenty-five years.`,context:`The nearly square canvas leaves out land and sky and shows only the water.`,source:`Art Institute of Chicago, collection entry for Water Lilies, 1933.1157`,url:`https://www.artic.edu/artworks/16568/water-lilies`},about:`Monet bought his property at Giverny in 1890 and made the water garden three years later. It became his main subject for the next two and a half decades, in around 250 paintings. This canvas belongs to a group made between 1903 and 1908, on the way to the large, near-abstract series he painted for the Orangerie, and technical examination found many changes made while he worked on it. Durand-Ruel showed it in Paris in 1909, and the Chicago collector Martin Ryerson bought it in 1914 for $5,000.`,learnMore:{label:`Art Institute of Chicago`,url:`https://www.artic.edu/artworks/16568/water-lilies`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:94.1,heightCm:89.9},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Art Institute of Chicago, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_Monet_-_Water_Lilies_-_1933.1157_-_Art_Institute_of_Chicago.jpg`,ratio:1.0392},{id:`vangogh-starry-night-over-the-rhone`,kind:`painting`,title:`Starry Night`,artist:`Vincent van Gogh`,date:`1888`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Musée d'Orsay, Paris`,caption:`The gaslights of Arles reflect in the Rhône under a starry sky, with two small figures of lovers on the bank. Often called Starry Night over the Rhône.`,line:{text:`I go outside at night to paint the stars.`,by:`Vincent van Gogh`,context:`to Theo, Arles, about 29 September 1888. The same letter calls this canvas “the starry sky at last, actually painted at night”.`,source:`Vincent van Gogh to Theo van Gogh, letter 691, Arles, on or about 29 September 1888, Vincent van Gogh: The Letters (Van Gogh Museum and Huygens ING)`,url:`https://vangoghletters.org/vg/letters/let691/letter.html`},about:`The gaslights of Arles reflect in the Rhône under a starry sky, with two lovers on the bank. In the letter that announced it, Van Gogh listed the colours: a green-blue sky, royal blue water, mauve land, a blue and violet town, and yellow gaslight whose reflections run from red gold to green bronze. The Great Bear is in the sky. Theo showed it with Irises at the Salon des Indépendants in September 1889, where the critic Félix Fénéon said its stars had been applied straight from the tube.`,learnMore:{label:`Musée d'Orsay`,url:`https://www.musee-orsay.fr/en/artworks/la-nuit-etoilee-78696`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:92,heightCm:73},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Musée d'Orsay, Paris, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Starry_Night_Over_the_Rhone.jpg`,ratio:1.2901,night:!0},{id:`vangogh-almond-blossom`,kind:`painting`,title:`Almond Blossom`,artist:`Vincent van Gogh`,date:`1890`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam`,caption:`Branches of white almond blossom against a blue sky, painted as a gift for a newborn nephew.`,line:{text:`Large branches of white almond blossom against a blue sky.`,by:`Vincent van Gogh`,context:`to his mother, 19 February 1890, of the picture he had begun for Theo’s son, born 31 January, to hang in the parents’ bedroom.`,source:`Vincent van Gogh to Anna van Gogh-Carbentus, letter 855, Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, 19 February 1890, Vincent van Gogh: The Letters (Van Gogh Museum and Huygens ING)`,url:`https://vangoghletters.org/vg/letters/let855/letter.html`},about:`Theo and his wife Jo had just had a son and named him Vincent Willem after his uncle. Almond trees flower early in spring, which made them a sign of new life; Van Gogh took the subject, the bold outlines and the placing of the tree from Japanese prints. In March he told Theo it was perhaps the most patiently worked, best thing he had done, painted with calm and a greater sureness of touch. The nephew it was made for went on to found the Van Gogh Museum.`,learnMore:{label:`Van Gogh Museum`,url:`https://www.vangoghmuseum.nl/en/collection/s0176V1962`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:92.4,heightCm:73.3},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam (Vincent van Gogh Foundation), public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amandelbloesem_-_s0176V1962_-_Van_Gogh_Museum.jpg`,ratio:1.2657},{id:`vangogh-irises`,kind:`painting`,title:`Irises`,artist:`Vincent van Gogh`,date:`1889`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles`,caption:`A bed of irises crowds the frame, painted from life in the garden of the asylum at Saint-Rémy.`,line:{text:`They’ve placed it on the narrow side of the room and it strikes you from a long way off. It’s a fine study, full of air and life.`,by:`Theo van Gogh`,context:`to Vincent, 5 September 1889, after hanging it at the Salon des Indépendants in Paris.`,source:`Theo van Gogh to Vincent van Gogh, letter 799, Paris, 5 September 1889, Vincent van Gogh: The Letters (Van Gogh Museum and Huygens ING)`,url:`https://vangoghletters.org/vg/letters/let799/letter.html`},about:`Van Gogh entered the asylum at Saint-Rémy in May 1889 and began this picture within his first week there. There are no known drawings for it, and he thought of it as a study. The cropped view and broad areas of strong colour probably owe something to Japanese woodblock prints. Its first owner was the critic Octave Mirbeau, one of Van Gogh’s earliest supporters, who bought it in 1891.`,learnMore:{label:`Getty`,url:`https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103JNH`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:94.3,heightCm:74.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles, CC0 (Open Content Program)`,sourceUrl:`https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103JNH`,ratio:1.2666},{id:`vangogh-wheatfield-under-thunderclouds`,kind:`painting`,title:`Wheatfield under Thunderclouds`,artist:`Vincent van Gogh`,date:`1890`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam`,caption:`A wheatfield spreads out under a dark sky near Auvers-sur-Oise, painted in the last weeks of Van Gogh's life.`,line:{text:`They’re immense stretches of wheatfields under turbulent skies, and I made a point of trying to express sadness, extreme loneliness.`,by:`Vincent van Gogh`,context:`to Theo and Jo, about 10 July 1890, of three canvases of the fields near Auvers. The editors think this one and Wheatfield with Crows are meant.`,source:`Vincent van Gogh to Theo van Gogh and Jo van Gogh-Bonger, letter 898, Auvers-sur-Oise, on or about 10 July 1890, with note 4, Vincent van Gogh: The Letters (Van Gogh Museum and Huygens ING)`,url:`https://vangoghletters.org/vg/letters/let898/letter.html`},about:`A wheatfield spreads out under a dark sky near Auvers-sur-Oise, painted in the last weeks of Van Gogh’s life. The composition is two horizontal bands, field and sky, on a canvas twice as wide as it is tall. The feeling was not only dark: in the same letter he hoped the canvases would tell Theo and Jo what he could not say in words, what he found healthy and fortifying about the countryside. Daubigny’s Garden, painted in the same days, has the same long format.`,learnMore:{label:`Van Gogh Museum`,url:`https://www.vangoghmuseum.nl/en/collection/s0106V1962`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:101.3,heightCm:50.4},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam (Vincent van Gogh Foundation), public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vincent_van_Gogh_-_Wheatfield_under_thunderclouds_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:2.0348},{id:`cezanne-montagne-sainte-victoire`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Montagne Sainte-Victoire with a Large Pine`,artist:`Paul Cézanne`,date:`c. 1887`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Courtauld, London`,caption:`The mountain near Aix-en-Provence rises beyond a wide valley, framed by the branches of a pine in the foreground.`,line:{text:`Cézanne gave it to the young poet Joachim Gasquet. It is one of the very few paintings he signed after 1880.`,context:`When first shown, at an exhibition of amateur artists in Aix, it met with incomprehension.`,source:`The Courtauld, collection highlight for Montagne Sainte-Victoire with Large Pine, P.1934.SC.55`,url:`https://courtauld.ac.uk/highlights/montagne-sainte-victoire-with-large-pine/`},about:`The mountain’s broken summit dominates the country around Cézanne’s home town of Aix-en-Provence, and he painted it from different viewpoints all his career. This is the most monumental and highly finished of three canvases of this view. Greens and yellows lead to the mountain, painted in cool blues and pinks, and the pine branches follow its outline. The only sign of modern life is on the right: a railway viaduct and the steam of a passing train.`,learnMore:{label:`The Courtauld`,url:`https://courtauld.ac.uk/highlights/montagne-sainte-victoire-with-large-pine/`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:92.3,heightCm:66.8},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`The Courtauld, London (Samuel Courtauld Trust), public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cezanne_-_Mont_Sainte-Victoire,_Courtauld.jpg`,ratio:1.3813},{id:`klimt-the-kiss`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Kiss`,artist:`Gustav Klimt`,date:`1908–09`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Belvedere, Vienna`,caption:`A couple embrace, wrapped in robes covered with gold ornament, on a square canvas 180 cm wide.`,line:{text:`The Austrian Ministry of Art bought it in 1908 at the Kunstschau in Vienna, where it was first shown as Liebespaar (Lovers).`,context:`A museum catalogue first called it The Kiss in autumn 1909.`,source:`Belvedere, Vienna, Sammlung Online, Der Kuss (Liebespaar), Inv.-Nr. 912`,url:`https://sammlung.belvedere.at/objects/6678/der-kuss-liebespaar`},about:`A couple embrace at the edge of a flower meadow, wrapped in robes covered with ornament, on a square canvas 180 cm wide. Klimt used real gold, silver and platinum leaf. The Belvedere dates the picture 1908, completed 1909, and the ministry paid 25,000 kronen for it. It is the high point of Klimt’s golden period, whose first idea came to him on a journey to Ravenna in 1903 to see the Byzantine mosaics.`,learnMore:{label:`Belvedere`,url:`https://sammlung.belvedere.at/objects/6678/der-kuss-liebespaar`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:180,heightCm:180},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Belvedere, Vienna, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klimt_-_The_Kiss.jpg`,ratio:.9781},{id:`cassatt-little-girl-blue-armchair`,kind:`painting`,title:`Little Girl in a Blue Armchair`,artist:`Mary Cassatt`,date:`1878`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`National Gallery of Art, Washington`,caption:`A small girl sprawls in a large blue armchair in a bright room of matching chairs, while a little dog sleeps on the chair opposite.`,line:{text:`Degas worked on the background himself. Infrared shows her single flat back wall and the corner he added.`,context:`Cassatt said so in a letter of about 1903; the imaging was done for the National Gallery’s 2014 Degas/Cassatt exhibition.`,source:`National Gallery of Art, Degas/Cassatt exhibition brochure, 2014, sidebar “Unraveling Cassatt’s Little Girl in a Blue Armchair”`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/sites/default/files/2025-06/degas-cassatt-brochure.pdf`},about:`A small girl sprawls in a large blue armchair in a bright room of matching chairs, while a little dog sleeps on the chair opposite. The girl was the daughter of friends of Degas; the dog is Cassatt’s own Brussels griffon, which Degas helped her find. Adding the corner meant Cassatt had to rework the couch in the centre, and infrared shows she tried the dog on the floor before returning it to its chair. She showed the picture at her debut with the Impressionists in 1879.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery of Art`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/61368-little-girl-blue-armchair`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:129.8,heightCm:89.5},licence:`cc0`,credit:`National Gallery of Art, Washington, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/61368-little-girl-blue-armchair`,ratio:1.4925},{id:`van-gogh-wheat-field-cypresses`,kind:`painting`,title:`Wheat Field with Cypresses`,artist:`Vincent van Gogh`,date:`1889`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A field of ripe wheat under a sky of swirling white clouds, with tall dark cypresses on the right and blue hills beyond.`,line:{text:`It’s beautiful as regards lines and proportions, like an Egyptian obelisk.`,by:`Vincent van Gogh`,context:`of the cypress, to Theo, Saint-Rémy, 25 June 1889.`,source:`Vincent van Gogh to Theo van Gogh, letter 783, Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, 25 June 1889, Vincent van Gogh: The Letters (Van Gogh Museum and Huygens ING)`,url:`https://vangoghletters.org/vg/letters/let783/letter.html`},about:`A field of ripe wheat under swirling white clouds, with dark cypresses on the right and blue hills beyond, painted out of doors near Saint-Rémy in the summer of 1889. The brushwork changes with each part of the scene: broad curves in the sky, flickering strokes in the cypresses, hatching in the wheat. When the Met cleaned the picture in 1993, the conservator found a wheat husk embedded in the paint. In September Van Gogh made two studio versions, one now in the National Gallery, London.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436535`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:93.4,heightCm:73.2},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436535`,ratio:1.2735},{id:`cezanne-mont-sainte-victoire-viaduct`,kind:`painting`,title:`Mont Sainte-Victoire and the Viaduct of the Arc River Valley`,artist:`Paul Cézanne`,date:`1882–85`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Mont Sainte-Victoire rises above the Arc River valley near Aix-en-Provence, seen past a tall pine, with fields and a railway viaduct on the plain.`,line:{text:`Cézanne painted it from the hill behind his sister’s house at Montbriand. The wall of the neighbouring farm is just visible.`,context:`The viaduct on the plain carried the railway across the Arc valley.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for Mont Sainte-Victoire and the Viaduct of the Arc River Valley, 29.100.64`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435877`},about:`Mont Sainte-Victoire rises above the Arc valley near Aix-en-Provence, seen past a tall pine, with fields and a railway viaduct on the plain. The viaduct recalls a Roman aqueduct, as in the paintings of Poussin; Cézanne wanted to make of Impressionism something solid and durable, like the art of museums. Depth is built from horizontal planes laid one behind another. The dealer Ambroise Vollard bought it from Cézanne for 250 francs and sold it in 1901 to Henry and Louisine Havemeyer, whose bequest brought it to the Met in 1929.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435877`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:81.6,heightCm:65.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435877`,ratio:1.2452}],t=[{id:`vermeer-milkmaid`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Milkmaid`,artist:`Johannes Vermeer`,date:`c. 1660`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam`,caption:`A maidservant pours milk from a pitcher, absorbed in her work in a bright, still room.`,line:{text:`At the lower right Vermeer first painted a fire basket for keeping a newborn warm, then covered it with the foot stove and the tiles.`,context:`Found in scans the Rijksmuseum announced in 2022. Vermeer’s own estate inventory lists such a basket.`,source:`Rijksmuseum, press release, 8 September 2022, Rijksmuseum reveals major discoveries on Vermeer’s painting The Milkmaid`,url:`https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/press/press-releases/rijksmuseum-reveals-major-discoveries-on-vermeer-s-painting-the-milkmaid`},about:`A maidservant pours milk from a jug in a plain room lit from a window at the left. The light on the bread and the jugs is built from hundreds of small coloured dots. Scans published in 2022 showed that Vermeer first sketched a rack of jugs on the wall behind her head, and a quick, thick line of black paint lies under her arm, a sign that he laid in light and dark before adding detail. The Rijksmuseum bought the painting in 1908 with the support of the Vereniging Rembrandt.`,learnMore:{label:`Rijksmuseum`,url:`https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/object/The-Milkmaid--42dd0e658c2979aec8e144d2357c55c0`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:41,heightCm:45.5},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/object/The-Milkmaid--42dd0e658c2979aec8e144d2357c55c0`,ratio:.898},{id:`rembrandt-night-watch`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Night Watch`,artist:`Rembrandt van Rijn`,date:`1642`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam`,caption:`Captain Frans Banninck Cocq tells his lieutenant to start his company of Amsterdam civic guardsmen marching.`,line:{text:`So powerful, that in the opinion of some, all the other pieces in the hall stand beside it like playing cards.`,by:`Samuel van Hoogstraten`,context:`Rembrandt’s former pupil, in his treatise on painting, 1678. He added that he would have preferred more light in it.`,source:`Samuel van Hoogstraten, Inleyding tot de Hooge Schoole der Schilderkonst (1678), p. 176, translated by Benjamin Binstock for the Columbia Art Humanities Primary Source Reader, reading 31`,url:`https://projects.mcah.columbia.edu/arthumanities/websites/remmon/pdf/art_hum_reading_31.pdf`},about:`Captain Frans Banninck Cocq tells his lieutenant to set the company marching, and around them the men load, fire and clean their weapons. It was the first group portrait in which the sitters are doing something. The shadow of the captain’s raised hand falls on the lieutenant’s coat, with the arms of Amsterdam between the thumb and fingers. The name Night Watch first appears in a document of 1797; in 1715 the canvas had been cut on four sides to fit between two doors in the Town Hall, and the pieces have never been found.`,learnMore:{label:`Rijksmuseum`,url:`https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/object/The-Night-Watch-Militia-Company-of-District-II-under-the-Command-of-Captain-Frans-Banninck-Cocq--3137deb45cd7765f9a76084a16c99544`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:453.5,heightCm:379.5},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/object/The-Night-Watch-Militia-Company-of-District-II-under-the-Command-of-Captain-Frans-Banninck-Cocq--3137deb45cd7765f9a76084a16c99544`,ratio:1.2045},{id:`bruegel-harvesters`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Harvesters`,artist:`Pieter Bruegel the Elder`,date:`1565`,medium:`Oil on oak`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Peasants cut wheat, gather fruit and rest over a midday meal under a pear tree on a hazy late-summer day.`,line:{text:`A wisp of green foliage seen through the path in the wheat field is Bruegel’s fingerprint.`,context:`From the Met’s technical notes on the painting, 2018. He applied the leaves of the largest trees with something other than a brush, perhaps a sponge.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, technical notes by Sophie Scully, 2018, on the object page for The Harvesters (19.164)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435809`},about:`Peasants cut wheat, carry sheaves and eat under a pear tree on a hazy late-summer day. The panel was one of six pictures of the seasons that the Antwerp merchant Niclaes Jongelinck commissioned for his country house, Bruegel’s only undisputed series. The Met bought it in 1919 for a low price while it was hidden under dirt, discoloured varnish and old repairs; cleaning uncovered the signature and the date 1565. Oil paint grows more transparent with age, so Bruegel’s rough chalk underdrawing now shows through in places.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435809`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:162,heightCm:119},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435809`,ratio:1.3567},{id:`van-eyck-arnolfini-portrait`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Arnolfini Portrait`,artist:`Jan van Eyck`,date:`1434`,medium:`Oil on wood (probably oak)`,place:`The National Gallery, London`,caption:`A richly dressed couple, probably an Italian merchant in Bruges and his wife, stand in a reception room with a convex mirror between them.`,line:{text:`Johannes de Eyck fuit hic. 1434`,by:`Jan van Eyck`,context:`painted on the wall above the mirror: Jan van Eyck was here, 1434. The mirror shows two men entering the room, and one may be the painter.`,source:`The National Gallery, London, in-depth entry for The Arnolfini Portrait (NG186)`,url:`https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/jan-van-eyck-the-arnolfini-portrait`},about:`A couple, probably an Italian merchant in Bruges and his wife, stand in a reception room with a convex mirror between them. The woman is not pregnant; she holds up the heavy fabric of her gown in front of her, as ladies commonly did. Infrared images show that the dog, the chandelier, the chair, the beads and the shoes were left out of the first underdrawing and painted in late. The room could not exist as shown, and for all its precision the picture was painted quickly, with fingers and brush handles as well as brush tips.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery`,url:`https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/jan-van-eyck-the-arnolfini-portrait`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:60,heightCm:82.2},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`The National Gallery, London, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Eyck_-_Arnolfini_Portrait.jpg`,ratio:.731},{id:`durer-rhinoceros`,kind:`print`,title:`The Rhinoceros`,artist:`Albrecht Dürer`,date:`1515`,medium:`Woodcut`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`An Indian rhinoceros, shown covered in what look like plates of armour.`,line:{text:`Dürer never saw the animal. He worked from a Portuguese newsletter sent to Nuremberg and some kind of sketch.`,context:`His own drawing for the woodcut is in the British Museum.`,source:`British Museum, curator’s comments on its impression of The Rhinoceros (1895,0122.714)`,url:`https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/P_1895-0122-714`},about:`The animal was a gift from Sultan Muzafar II of Gujarat to the governor of Portuguese India, who sent it to King Manuel I. It reached Lisbon on 20 May 1515, the first rhinoceros in Europe alive since the third century. Manuel sent it on as a gift to the Pope, but it drowned when its ship sank on the way. The printed text above the image gives the year as 1513 by mistake, and of the seven editions printed from the block only the first appeared in Dürer’s lifetime.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/356497`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:29.9,heightCm:23.8},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/356497`,ratio:1.2499},{id:`velazquez-juan-de-pareja`,kind:`painting`,title:`Juan de Pareja (ca. 1608–1670)`,artist:`Diego Velázquez`,date:`1650`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A portrait of the painter Juan de Pareja, made in Rome while he was enslaved in Velázquez's household.`,line:{text:`Everything else looked like painting, this alone like reality.`,by:`Antonio Palomino`,context:`reporting what the painters said when Velázquez showed it in Rome in 1650. Velázquez freed Pareja in 1654; Pareja became a painter in Madrid.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, catalogue entry for Juan de Pareja (1971.86), quoting Antonio Palomino, El museo pictórico, y escala óptica, vol. 3, Madrid, 1724`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/437869`},about:`Pareja was born around 1608 in Antequera in southern Spain and was enslaved in Velázquez’s studio and household for at least two decades. He went with Velázquez to Italy in 1649, and the portrait was hung under the portico of the Pantheon in Rome on 19 March 1650, at the yearly exhibition for the feast of Saint Joseph. Pareja’s own Calling of Saint Matthew of 1661, now in the Prado, includes a self-portrait. At Sir William Hamilton’s sale in London in 1801 the portrait sold for about £41; the Met bought it at Christie’s in 1970.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/437869`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:69.9,heightCm:81.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/437869`,ratio:.8637},{id:`la-tour-penitent-magdalen`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Penitent Magdalen`,artist:`Georges de La Tour`,date:`c. 1640`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Mary Magdalen sits in the dark beside a candle, a skull and a mirror, turning from worldly life.`,line:{text:`The two flames are one candle: the second is its reflection in the mirror. The picture is also called the Magdalen with Two Flames.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, catalogue entry for The Penitent Magdalen (1978.517)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436839`},about:`Mary Magdalen sits in the dark with a skull on her lap, before a candle and a silver mirror. The jewellery she has given up lies on the table and the floor; the earrings match those worn by a seated woman in La Tour’s Cheat with the Ace of Clubs in Fort Worth. He painted four Magdalens accepted as his, each on a different side of her reformed life. This one shows her conversion, and the others are in Washington, Paris and Los Angeles; no document dates it, and it is placed about 1640.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436839`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:102.2,heightCm:133.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436839`,ratio:.7038,night:!0},{id:`van-eyck-crucifixion-last-judgment`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Crucifixion; The Last Judgment`,artist:`Jan van Eyck`,date:`c. 1436–38`,medium:`Oil on canvas, transferred from wood`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Two tall, narrow panels: Christ dies on the cross before Jerusalem, and in the Last Judgment Death spreads his wings over the damned in hell.`,line:{text:`At the right edge of the Crucifixion a waning gibbous moon hangs at midday, placing the scene hours before Christ’s death.`,context:`The Gospel of Mark puts his death at the third hour after noon.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, catalogue entry for The Crucifixion; The Last Judgment (33.92ab)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436282`},about:`Two tall, narrow panels: Christ dies on the cross before Jerusalem, and in the Last Judgment Death spreads his wings over the damned. The grieving figures at the foot of the cross have red-rimmed eyes made with fine strokes of red paint, and even the smallest faces have their own expressions. The frames are the originals; a treatment in 2015–16 removed later brass leaf and found a Middle Dutch translation of their Latin verses in white paint. In 1867, while in the Hermitage, the paintings were moved from their wooden panels onto canvas.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436282`},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436282`,ratio:.8319},{id:`david-the-death-of-socrates`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Death of Socrates`,artist:`Jacques Louis David`,date:`1787`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Condemned in Athens in 399 BC, Socrates reaches for the cup of hemlock and points upward as his followers grieve.`,line:{text:`The best thing is the Death of Socrates by David, and a superb one it is.`,by:`Thomas Jefferson`,context:`in a letter from Paris to the painter John Trumbull, 30 August 1787, the year it was shown at the Salon.`,source:`Thomas Jefferson to John Trumbull, Paris, 30 August 1787, in The Papers of Thomas Jefferson, vol. 12 (Founders Online, National Archives)`,url:`https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Jefferson/01-12-02-0076`},about:`Condemned in Athens in 399 BC, Socrates reaches for the cup of hemlock and points upward while his followers grieve. The figure seated at the foot of the bed is usually taken to be Plato, who was not there on the day; David read Plato’s Phaedo and consulted the classical scholar Jean Félicissime Adry. Infrared imaging shows that he moved the chain of the shackles from in front of the bed leg to behind the bed, and first painted Socrates’s wrist closer to the cup. One of the Trudaine brothers commissioned the picture; both brothers and their friend the poet André Chénier died by the guillotine in 1794.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436105`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:196.2,heightCm:129.5},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436105`,ratio:1.5137},{id:`goya-manuel-osorio-manrique-de-zuniga`,kind:`painting`,title:`Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga (1784–1792)`,artist:`Francisco de Goya`,date:`1787–88`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Manuel, the small son of the Count of Altamira, stands in a red suit with a pet magpie on a string, a cage of finches and three watching cats.`,line:{text:`Manuel died at eight, in 1792, four years after Goya painted him.`,context:`He was born on 11 April 1784 and died on 12 June 1792; the Met dates the portrait to 1787–88.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, catalogue entry for Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga (49.7.41)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436545`},about:`Manuel, a son of the Count of Altamira, stands in a red suit holding a magpie on a string; the bird has Goya’s calling card in its beak, a cage of finches sits at his feet and three cats watch from the shadow. Caged birds are usually read as symbols of the soul and of innocence, and the cats as forces of evil. In January 1787 Goya was paid for a portrait of the count for the Banco de San Carlos, and its success led the count to order portraits of his wife, daughter and two sons. The playwright Henri Bernstein owned the picture from at least 1903 to 1925, and a copy of it stood in the set of his play La Galerie des Glaces in 1924.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436545`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:101.6,heightCm:127},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/436545`,ratio:.7372}],n=[{id:`turner-fighting-temeraire`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Fighting Temeraire`,artist:`J. M. W. Turner`,date:`1839`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The National Gallery, London`,caption:`A small steam tug tows the old warship Temeraire up the Thames to be broken up, under a blazing sunset.`,line:{text:`The old Teméraire is dragged to her last home by a little, spiteful, diabolical steamer.`,by:`W. M. Thackeray`,context:`reviewing the Royal Academy in Fraser's Magazine, June 1839, under the name Michael Angelo Titmarsh.`,source:`W. M. Thackeray (as Michael Angelo Titmarsh), review of the Royal Academy exhibition, Fraser's Magazine, June 1839; transcription in the Lancaster University Ruskin Programme notes (the Victorian Web transcription spells the ship Téméraire)`,url:`https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/fass/ruskin/empi/notes/ymisc10.htm`},about:`The 98-gun Temeraire fought beside Nelson's Victory at Trafalgar in 1805. In 1838 its stripped hull was sold for £5,530 to a shipbreaker at Rotherhithe and towed up the Thames. Turner probably did not see the tow: he gave the ship back its masts, painted its black and yellow hull white and gold, and set the sunset behind a ship heading west, where it could not be. He called the picture his Darling, refused offers to buy it, and it came to the National Gallery with his bequest after his death in 1851.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery`,url:`https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/joseph-mallord-william-turner-the-fighting-temeraire`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:121.6,heightCm:90.7},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`The National Gallery, London, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turner%2C_J._M._W._-_The_Fighting_T%C3%A9m%C3%A9raire_tugged_to_her_last_Berth_to_be_broken.jpg`,ratio:1.3468},{id:`turner-venice-salute`,kind:`painting`,title:`Venice, from the Porch of Madonna della Salute`,artist:`J. M. W. Turner`,date:`c. 1835`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`The mouth of the Grand Canal in Venice, seen from the steps of Santa Maria della Salute, its palaces reflected in the water.`,line:{text:`Engravings of 1838 and 1850 show his initials on a plank at lower left. They are not on the painting and, as far as is known, never were.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, catalogue entry for the painting (object 437853), Signature / Inscription`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/437853`},about:`The view is the mouth of the Grand Canal from the steps of Santa Maria della Salute, under Turner's own title. He based it on a slight pencil drawing from his first visit to Venice in 1819, painted it after his second visit in 1833, and showed it at the Royal Academy in 1835. He changed the scale and the topography to heighten the effect. When the first owner sold it in 1860, John Ruskin's father proposed that twenty gentlemen buy it and give it to the Louvre, since Turner was so little known on the Continent.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/437853`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:122.2,heightCm:91.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/437853`,ratio:1.337},{id:`friedrich-wanderer-sea-of-fog`,kind:`painting`,title:`Wanderer above the Sea of Fog`,artist:`Caspar David Friedrich`,date:`c. 1817`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg`,caption:`A man in city clothes stands on a rocky summit with his back to us, looking out over mountains rising from fog.`,line:{text:`The view is assembled from studies: the rock from a drawing of 3 June 1813, the cone-shaped mountain from a study of 1808.`,context:`The rock was drawn in Saxon Switzerland; the mountain is the Rosenberg in Bohemia, studied in May 1808.`,source:`Hamburger Kunsthalle, online collection entry HK-5161 (text by Markus Bertsch, exhibition catalogue, 2023)`,url:`https://online-sammlung.hamburger-kunsthalle.de/de/objekt/HK-5161`},about:`A man in city clothes stands on a rocky summit with his back turned, looking over mountains that rise from fog. The picture is unusual for Friedrich: one of his few upright canvases, with a single figure placed large in the centre. The viewer is never shown what he sees, and the museum reads the painting as a reflection on looking itself. Despite many recent attempts, no reliable evidence identifies the man.`,learnMore:{label:`Hamburger Kunsthalle`,url:`https://online-sammlung.hamburger-kunsthalle.de/en/objekt/HK-5161`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:74.8,heightCm:94.8},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Wikimedia Commons, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caspar_David_Friedrich_-_Wanderer_above_the_Sea_of_Fog.jpeg`,ratio:.7796},{id:`bierstadt-rocky-mountains-landers-peak`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Rocky Mountains, Lander's Peak`,artist:`Albert Bierstadt`,date:`1863`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A Shoshone camp in a wide valley below the snow-capped Wind River Range, in present-day Wyoming.`,line:{text:`Bierstadt named the peak after Frederick Lander, who led the 1859 survey he joined and died in the Civil War.`,context:`Lander's survey reached the Wind River Range in summer 1859; Lander died in 1862.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline entry for the painting (07.123), archived 2017; Met collection entry 10154`,url:`https://web.archive.org/web/20170201125710/http://www.metmuseum.org/toah/works-of-art/07.123/`},about:`A Shoshone camp lies in a wide valley below the snow-covered Wind River Range, in present-day Wyoming. Bierstadt saw the range on the 1859 survey and painted this canvas, about 1.9 by 3.1 metres, in New York in 1863. At the 1864 Metropolitan Fair it hung opposite Church's Heart of the Andes as a deliberate rival, and in 1865 James McHenry, an American living in London, bought it for $25,000; Bierstadt later bought it back. The Met notes that the picture advertised the West to white settlers while ignoring the Indigenous peoples shown in the foreground.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/10154`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:306.7,heightCm:186.7},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/10154`,ratio:1.6686},{id:`church-heart-of-the-andes`,kind:`painting`,title:`Heart of the Andes`,artist:`Frederic Edwin Church`,date:`1859`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A composite view of the Ecuadorian Andes, from a tropical valley to a snow-covered peak, built from many sketches.`,line:{text:`I have seen it several times, but it is always a new picture.`,by:`Mark Twain`,context:`in a letter to his brother Orion, 18 March 1861, after taking an opera glass to it in St. Louis.`,source:`Samuel L. Clemens to Orion Clemens, 18 March 1861, St. Louis, Mo. (UCCL 00022), Mark Twain Project Online`,url:`https://www.marktwainproject.org/letters/uccl00022/`},about:`Church followed the naturalist Alexander von Humboldt, whose book Cosmos urged artists to paint equatorial South America, and travelled to Ecuador in 1853 and 1857. The painting joins many sketches into one view, from a tropical valley to a snow-covered peak. In 1859 he showed it alone in a darkened room, in a large frame like a window, lit by gas jets behind silver reflectors, and advised visitors to bring opera glasses to study the plants. Twelve thousand people came in its first three weeks in New York, and it then toured Britain and seven other American cities for two years.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/10481`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:302.9,heightCm:168},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/10481`,ratio:1.8245},{id:`homer-breezing-up`,kind:`painting`,title:`Breezing Up (A Fair Wind)`,artist:`Winslow Homer`,date:`1873–1876`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`National Gallery of Art, Washington`,caption:`A man and several boys sail a small catboat through choppy green water on a bright, windy day.`,line:{text:`A fifth figure, a boy, once sat in the bow. Homer painted him out and put an anchor there.`,context:`Infrared images show the change.`,source:`National Gallery of Art, collection entry 1943.13.1 ("an anchor replaces a figure in the bow"); the boy identified in infrared imaging, The Christian Science Monitor, 14 September 2005`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/30228-breezing-fair-wind`},about:`A man and three boys sail a small catboat through choppy green water on a bright, windy day. Homer worked from sketches and watercolours made at Gloucester, Massachusetts, in 1873 and finished the painting in 1876, refining the composition over those three years. He showed it in the centennial year at the National Academy of Design as A Fair Wind; by 1887 it was exhibited as Breezing Up. Critics read the anchor as a symbol of hope and the boy's forward gaze as hopeful for the nation after the Civil War.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery of Art`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/30228-breezing-fair-wind`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:97,heightCm:61.5},licence:`cc0`,credit:`National Gallery of Art, Washington, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/30228-breezing-fair-wind`,ratio:1.5886},{id:`friedrich-two-men-contemplating-the-moon`,kind:`painting`,title:`Two Men Contemplating the Moon`,artist:`Caspar David Friedrich`,date:`c. 1825–30`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Two men stand on a rocky path beside a gnarled oak and a fir, watching the moon rise.`,line:{text:`Friedrich's doctor in Dresden took it from him in exchange for medical care; his family kept it until 1999.`,context:`Otto Friedrich Rosenberg's heirs sold it at Christie's, London, in 1999; the Met bought it in 2000.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the painting (object 438417), provenance`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/438417`},about:`Two men stand on a rocky path beside a gnarled oak and a fir, watching the moon rise. One has often been identified as Friedrich himself, the other as his student August Heinrich, who died in 1822; the German Romantics regarded the moon as an object of pious contemplation. This is the third version of the composition: the first was painted in 1819, while Heinrich was alive, the second about 1824, soon after his death. There is no underdrawing beneath the paint.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/438417`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:43.8,heightCm:34.9},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/438417`,ratio:1.2525,night:!0},{id:`whistler-falling-rocket`,kind:`painting`,title:`Nocturne in Black and Gold, the Falling Rocket`,artist:`James Abbott McNeill Whistler`,date:`1875`,medium:`Oil on panel`,place:`Detroit Institute of Arts, Detroit`,caption:`Sparks from a firework rocket drift down through the night sky over Cremorne Gardens, a pleasure garden by the Thames in Chelsea, London.`,line:{text:`No;—I ask it for the knowledge of a lifetime.`,by:`James McNeill Whistler`,context:`in court, 1878, asked if two hundred guineas was for two days' work. Ruskin had called the picture a pot of paint flung in the public's face; the jury gave Whistler a farthing.`,source:`James McNeill Whistler, The Gentle Art of Making Enemies (1890), 'The Action', Whistler's own account of Whistler v. Ruskin, November 1878; Project Gutenberg eBook 24650`,url:`https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24650/pg24650-images.html`},about:`Sparks from a rocket drift down over Cremorne Gardens, a pleasure garden by the Thames in Chelsea; the two lit towers are turrets of the fireworks platform. Whistler said the picture was not the portrait of a particular place but an artistic impression carried away, and the catalogue raisonné reads it as painted from memory. Over a thin reddish-brown base he used square brushes 7 to 14 mm wide, with much finer ones to set down each fleck of orange and green. It was the only one of his eight pictures at the Grosvenor Gallery in 1877 listed for sale.`,learnMore:{label:`Detroit Institute of Arts`,url:`https://dia.org/collection/nocturne-black-and-gold-falling-rocket-64931`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:46.7,heightCm:60.3},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Detroit Institute of Arts, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Whistler-Nocturne_in_black_and_gold.jpg`,ratio:.7526,night:!0},{id:`sargent-madame-x`,kind:`painting`,title:`Madame X (Virginie Amélie Avegno Gautreau)`,artist:`John Singer Sargent`,date:`1883–84`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A full-length portrait of Virginie Gautreau, a Louisiana-born woman known in Paris for her artful appearance, in a black evening dress.`,line:{text:`I suppose it is the best thing I have done.`,by:`John Singer Sargent`,context:`offering it to the Met in 1916, after keeping it for thirty years; he asked that her name not be used.`,source:`John Singer Sargent to Edward Robinson, 8 January 1916 (The Metropolitan Museum of Art Archives), transcribed in full in Stephanie L. Herdrich, 'From the Archives: How Madame X Came to the Met', metmuseum.org, 8 January 2016, note 2`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/how-madame-x-came-to-the-met`},about:`Virginie Gautreau, born in Louisiana, was known in Paris for her artful appearance. Sargent painted her without a commission, in a profile pose that recalls Titian, with a palette and brushwork taken from Velázquez. At the 1884 Salon, where the picture hung as Madame ***, the jewelled right strap had slipped from her shoulder and drew ridicule in the press; her mother asked Sargent to withdraw it, and he refused. He later repainted the strap, and a photograph of the painting as shown in 1884 survives in the Met's Watson Library.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/12127`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:109.9,heightCm:208.6},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/12127`,ratio:.5258},{id:`sorolla-fish-is-expensive`,kind:`painting`,title:`And They Still Say Fish Is Expensive!`,artist:`Joaquín Sorolla`,date:`1894`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid`,caption:`In the hold of a fishing boat, two old fishermen tend a young sailor hurt in an accident at sea.`,line:{text:`The title comes from the closing lines of Vicente Blasco Ibáñez's novel Flor de mayo, which he was writing while Sorolla painted this.`,context:`The novel was published in 1895; both men were from Valencia.`,source:`Museo Nacional del Prado, collection entry for the painting; the novel's ending checked in Flor de mayo (Valencia, Prometeo), Project Gutenberg eBook 41746`,url:`https://www.museodelprado.es/en/the-collection/art-work/and-they-still-say-fish-is-expensive/a4fcf4c7-4d54-4e50-9255-25b44f0e0416`},about:`In the hold of a fishing boat, two old fishermen tend a young sailor hurt in an accident at sea. Light falls through the open hatch; behind the men are the ladder they used to bring him down and a pile of silver fish from the day's catch. On the boy's bare chest hangs a medallion worn to protect fishermen from misfortune. Sorolla sent the picture to the 1895 National Exhibition in Madrid, where it won a first-class medal and was bought by the State.`,learnMore:{label:`Museo del Prado`,url:`https://www.museodelprado.es/en/the-collection/art-work/and-they-still-say-fish-is-expensive/a4fcf4c7-4d54-4e50-9255-25b44f0e0416`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:204,heightCm:151.5},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joaqu%C3%ADn_Sorolla_y_Bastida_-_A%C3%BAn_dicen_que_el_pescado_es_caro.jpg`,ratio:1.3259},{id:`hammershoi-interior-strandgade`,kind:`painting`,title:`Interior in Strandgade, Sunlight on the Floor`,artist:`Vilhelm Hammershøi`,date:`1901`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`SMK – National Gallery of Denmark, Copenhagen`,caption:`Sunlight from a window falls across the floor of the artist's flat at Strandgade 30 in Copenhagen, while his wife sits at a table.`,line:{text:`When it was cleaned in 2012, the conservator found more than forty kinds of white in it.`,context:`Cleaned for an exhibition; Hammershøi's palette was mostly black, white, grey and brown.`,source:`Statens Museum for Kunst, text for the painting (KMS3696) on Google Arts & Culture`,url:`https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/a-room-in-the-artist-s-home-in-strandgade-copenhagen-with-the-artist-s-wife-vilhelm-hammersh%C3%B8i/qAF9h8gI6HYvSw`},about:`Hammershøi lived at Strandgade 30 in Copenhagen from 1898 to 1908 and set about sixty-six interiors in the flat, almost half of what he painted in those years. Here sunlight from a window falls across the floor while his wife sits at a table. The paintings are not exact records of the rooms: he left out details such as door handles and kept some areas empty. In 1907 he said that a picture works best, in purely colouristic terms, the fewer colours it has.`,learnMore:{label:`SMK, National Gallery of Denmark`,url:`https://open.smk.dk/artwork/image/KMS3696`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:52,heightCm:46.5},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`SMK – National Gallery of Denmark, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://open.smk.dk/artwork/image/KMS3696`,ratio:1.1408},{id:`rousseau-repast-of-the-lion`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Repast of the Lion`,artist:`Henri Rousseau`,date:`c. 1907`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A lion seizes its prey among giant flowers and leaves in a jungle that Rousseau imagined in Paris.`,line:{text:`Rousseau never left France; the plants come from the Paris botanical gardens and the animals from illustrated books.`,context:`He worked from studies in the Jardin des Plantes and from popular journals and children's books.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the painting (object 438822); Roger Shattuck et al., Henri Rousseau, exh. cat. (MoMA, 1985), p. 11`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/438822`},about:`A lion seizes its prey among giant leaves and flowers in a jungle Rousseau imagined in Paris. The sun is white, which is unusual for him, and may be the moon; apart from a faded pink flower at the right and the animal's wounds there is no red in the picture. His nickname le Douanier is a misnomer: he worked for the Paris municipal toll service, and the story that he had been to Mexico with French troops was spread by Apollinaire and is false. Critics mocked him for years; in 1891 one wrote that he painted with his feet with his eyes closed.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/438822`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:160,heightCm:113.7},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/438822`,ratio:1.4248}],r=[{id:`hokusai-great-wave`,kind:`print`,title:`Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)`,artist:`Katsushika Hokusai`,culture:`Edo period, Japan`,date:`c. 1830–32`,medium:`Woodblock print; ink and color on paper. From Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A huge wave curls over three boats of rowers off the coast of Kanagawa.`,line:{text:`At one hundred and ten, every dot and every stroke will be as though alive.`,by:`Katsushika Hokusai`,context:`postscript to One Hundred Views of Mount Fuji, 1834, written at 75; he died at 89.`,source:`Hokusai, postscript to One Hundred Views of Mt. Fuji, vol. 1 (1834), in the translation of Henry D. Smith II, Hokusai: One Hundred Views of Mt. Fuji (1988), p. 7, as quoted by The Public Domain Review`,url:`https://publicdomainreview.org/collection/hokusai-warriors/`},about:`Mount Fuji sits as a small triangle inside the hollow of the breaking wave, and three boats of rowers are caught beneath it. The publisher Eijudo advertised the series for the New Year of 1831 and promoted its Prussian blue, a pigment newly available for the print market. Scientists at the Met found that the printers mixed Prussian blue with indigo for the dark stripes of the wave and then printed pure Prussian blue over them, so the foam, the bright blue and the deep blue sit at different heights in the paper. The Met's impression is probably one of the earliest printed.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/45434`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:37.9,heightCm:25.7},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/45434`,ratio:1.4877},{id:`hokusai-red-fuji`,kind:`print`,title:`South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji)`,artist:`Katsushika Hokusai`,culture:`Edo period, Japan`,date:`c. 1830–32`,medium:`Woodblock print; ink and color on paper. From Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Mount Fuji, coloured red, rises under rows of small white clouds in a clear blue sky.`,line:{text:`Hokusai was about seventy when the series appeared. It sold so well that the publisher extended it from thirty-six views to forty-six.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entries for the print (JP2960) and another impression (JP9)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/55736`},about:`The title names the weather, South Wind, Clear Sky, but the print is known as Red Fuji, after the colour the mountain is said to take at dawn in early autumn. There is no one in the picture; the forest at the foot of the mountain is reduced to tiny green triangles, and the clouds are patterned like white paper against the blue. Hokusai, born in 1760, lived until 1849.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/55736`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:37.8,heightCm:25.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/55736`,ratio:1.4604},{id:`hiroshige-sudden-shower-atake`,kind:`print`,title:`Sudden Shower over Shin-Ōhashi Bridge and Atake`,artist:`Utagawa Hiroshige`,culture:`Edo period, Japan`,date:`1857`,medium:`Woodblock print; ink and color on paper. From One Hundred Famous Views of Edo`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Heavy rain falls on people hurrying across a long wooden bridge over the Sumida River in Edo, now Tokyo.`,line:{text:`I envy the Japanese the extreme clarity that everything in their work has.`,by:`Vincent van Gogh`,context:`letter to Theo, September 1888. He had copied this print in oils in Paris the year before.`,source:`Vincent van Gogh to Theo van Gogh, Arles, Sunday 23 or Monday 24 September 1888, letter 686, Vincent van Gogh: The Letters (Van Gogh Museum and Huygens ING)`,url:`https://vangoghletters.org/vg/letters/let686/letter.html`},about:`The title names a yūdachi, a summer storm that darkens the sky late in the day, drops heavy rain and quickly clears. Six people on the bridge shelter under hats, mats and umbrellas, and below them one boatman poles a log raft down the Sumida River. The rain is printed as a net of fine crossing lines laid over the scene, a difficult cut for the block carver. Van Gogh's oil copy of 1887 made the colours stronger and filled a border with Japanese characters taken from other prints.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/55433`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:24.1,heightCm:34},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/55433`,ratio:.6865},{id:`hiroshige-plum-garden-kameido`,kind:`print`,title:`The Plum Garden at Kameido Shrine`,artist:`Utagawa Hiroshige`,culture:`Edo period, Japan`,date:`1857`,medium:`Woodblock print; ink and color on paper. From One Hundred Famous Views of Edo`,place:`Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam`,caption:`Visitors in a plum orchard are seen through the dark, twisting branches of an old plum tree, under a red sky.`,line:{text:`The tree in front, the Sleeping Dragon Plum, was the most famous tree in Edo. It survived until a flood killed it in 1910.`,source:`Brooklyn Museum, catalogue description for its impression of the print (30.1478.30)`,url:`https://www.brooklynmuseum.org/objects/4202`},about:`The low branches of the old plum went into the ground and came up again as new trunks, so the tree spread over an area about 15 metres square. One Edo guidebook said its double blossoms were so white in full bloom as to drive off the darkness, and its scent was said to have drawn the shogun Yoshimune as he passed nearby in the early 1700s. Van Gogh owned a copy and painted it in oil in 1887, the first of his three copies after Japanese prints, turning the black and grey trunk red and blue.`,learnMore:{label:`Rijksmuseum`,url:`https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/object/The-Plum-Garden-at-Kameido-Shrine--835003111f8a3f0b1d1e6532cad2d416`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:24.4,heightCm:36.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_pruimenboomgaard_te_Kameido_Kameido_umeyashiki_(titel_op_object)_Honderd_beroemde_gezichten_op_Edo_(serietitel)_Meisho_Edo_hyakkei_(serietitel_op_object),_RP-P-1956-743.jpg`,ratio:.6804},{id:`guo-xi-old-trees-level-distance`,kind:`painting`,title:`Old Trees, Level Distance`,artist:`Guo Xi`,culture:`Northern Song dynasty, China`,date:`c. 1080`,medium:`Handscroll; ink and color on silk`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`The final section of a long silk handscroll, where bare old trees and a pavilion stand on a misty river bank.`,line:{text:`The painting is 104 cm long, but the whole scroll is 854 cm. The rest is notes and seals added by friends, collectors and later viewers.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the painting (1981.276)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39668`},about:`This is the final section of a handscroll, which was not hung on a wall but unrolled from right to left, one shoulder-width at a time. Guo Xi was the leading landscape painter of the late eleventh century, and this is probably a late work, made for a fellow official about to retire. Two elderly figures walk toward the pavilion, perhaps to join colleagues saying goodbye to a friend. The Met reads the leafless trees and deepening mist as an autumn scene of parting.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39668`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:104.4,heightCm:35.6},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39668`,ratio:1.8739},{id:`fan-kuan-travelers-mountains-streams`,kind:`painting`,title:`Travelers among Mountains and Streams`,artist:`Fan Kuan`,culture:`Northern Song dynasty, China`,date:`10th–early 11th century`,medium:`Hanging scroll, ink and light colors on silk`,place:`National Palace Museum, Taipei`,caption:`A great mountain fills the upper half of a tall silk scroll, while a small mule train crosses the rocks below.`,line:{text:`Fan Kuan's signature, two characters, is hidden among the leaves to the right of the mule train.`,source:`National Palace Museum, Taipei, selection page for the painting (signature hidden among the trees to the lower right); National Palace Museum entry on Google Arts & Culture (to the right of the mule train)`,url:`https://theme.npm.edu.tw/exh104/npmforfun/en/Selection/Selection2.html`},about:`The mountain fills the upper half of a silk scroll just over two metres tall, and the mule train below is tiny beside it. Fan Kuan first followed the painter Li Cheng, then chose to learn from nature instead and lived in the forests near Mount Hua, watching how weather and seasons changed the scenery. He drew the slopes with jagged outlines and filled them with short dabs of the brush that look like raindrops. In early Northern Song landscapes the great central peak was compared to a ruler among his subjects.`,learnMore:{label:`National Palace Museum`,url:`https://theme.npm.edu.tw/exh104/npmforfun/en/Selection/Selection2.html`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:103.3,heightCm:206.3},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`National Palace Museum, Taipei, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fan_Kuan_-_Travelers_Among_Mountains_and_Streams_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg`,ratio:.4987},{id:`ma-yuan-plum-blossoms-moonlight`,kind:`painting`,title:`Viewing Plum Blossoms by Moonlight`,artist:`Ma Yuan`,culture:`Southern Song dynasty, China`,date:`early 13th century`,medium:`Fan mounted as an album leaf; ink and color on silk`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A gentleman in white sits beneath a cliff while a plum tree reaches out toward a pale moon in a misty sky.`,line:{text:`It was painted on an oval silk fan, later taken off its frame and mounted as an album leaf.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the painting (1986.493.2; medium: fan mounted as an album leaf) and the Heilbrunn Timeline essay Painting Formats in East Asian Art`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/painting-formats-in-east-asian-art`},about:`A gentleman in white sits beneath a cliff while a plum tree reaches toward a pale moon. Brown and black trees and rocks against grey cliffs give the misty air of an early spring evening, and a thatched roof marks the place as a garden. The small white figure balances the moon across the empty space; the Met compares the design to a yin-yang diagram and to a regulated Tang quatrain that sets a time, a place and a mood. Southern Song court painters often worked at this small size, and their paintings often dwell on passing moments.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/44638`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:26.7,heightCm:25.1},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/44638`,ratio:1.0703,night:!0},{id:`hiroshige-evening-snow-kanbara`,kind:`print`,title:`Evening Snow at Kanbara`,artist:`Utagawa Hiroshige`,culture:`Edo period, Japan`,date:`ca. 1833–34`,medium:`Woodblock print; ink and color on paper. From Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Snow falls on a mountain village at Kanbara in the evening as three travellers walk through it.`,line:{text:`The scene is imaginary. Records show Hiroshige passed through Kanbara in summer, and such snow is unusual there.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the print (JP2492)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/56915`},about:`Kanbara was one of the fifty-three stopping places on the Tōkaidō road from Edo, now Tokyo, to Kyoto, and this series of fifty-five prints made Hiroshige famous. Three people trudge through the snow and leave footprints, one with his head under a half-open umbrella. In the first impressions the heavily inked band sits at the top of the sky. Later printings moved it down behind the hills and trees for a stronger night effect, and by the last impressions the worn blocks had lost the footprints.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/56915`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:34.9,heightCm:22.5},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/56915`,ratio:1.5042,night:!0},{id:`korin-irises-yatsuhashi`,kind:`painting`,title:`Irises at Yatsuhashi (Eight Bridges)`,artist:`Ogata Kōrin`,culture:`Edo period, Japan`,date:`after 1709`,medium:`Pair of six-panel folding screens; ink and color on gold leaf on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Blue irises grow beside a zigzag wooden bridge on one of a pair of gold folding screens.`,line:{text:`The poem the irises recall is an acrostic: the first syllables of its five lines spell kakitsubata, the word for irises.`,context:`From The Ise Stories, in which the exiled hero stops at Yatsuhashi and writes a love poem.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the screens (53.7.1, .2)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39664`},about:`The screens refer to an episode in The Ise Stories: the hero, exiled from Kyoto after an affair with a court lady, stops at Yatsuhashi, where a stream branches into eight channels, each with its own bridge. Kōrin signed the right screen Seisei Kōrin and the left one Hokkyō Kōrin, and stamped both with the seal Masatoki. He painted another pair of iris screens, now a National Treasure at the Nezu Museum, and in 2012 the two pairs were shown together for the first time since 1915. The Rinpa style takes its name from him.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39664`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:352.4,heightCm:163.7},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39664`,ratio:2.0619},{id:`qu-ding-summer-mountains`,kind:`painting`,title:`Summer Mountains`,artist:`Attributed to Qu Ding`,culture:`Northern Song dynasty, China`,date:`ca. 1050`,medium:`Handscroll; ink and color on silk`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A great central peak rises above misty forests as travellers make their way to a temple in the hills.`,line:{text:`In 1748 the Qianlong emperor wrote a poem on the scroll, asking how it would feel to lean on a railing of its pavilions and look out.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the scroll (1973.120.1), inscriptions`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39915`},about:`The scroll is read from right to left. Travellers make their way toward a temple retreat where visitors sit together enjoying the view, and the mist in the forests marks a midsummer evening. There is no signature, and an old frontispiece names Yan Wengui instead. Seals of Emperor Huizong (r. 1101–25) are on the scroll, and his painting catalogue lists three works called Summer Scenery by Qu Ding, a painter otherwise unknown.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39915`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:115.3,heightCm:45.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39915`,ratio:2.5925},{id:`dong-yuan-riverbank`,kind:`painting`,title:`Riverbank`,artist:`Attributed to Dong Yuan`,culture:`Southern Tang dynasty, China`,date:`Southern Tang dynasty (937–76)`,medium:`Hanging scroll; ink and color on silk`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A scholar's home by a river sits below rounded hills and tall mountains in a scroll attributed to the 10th-century painter Dong Yuan.`,line:{text:`In 1999 the historian James Cahill argued at the Met that it is a forgery by Zhang Daqian; the Met holds it is tenth century.`,context:`Symposium at the Met, 11 December 1999. Wen Fong of the Met replied that physical and stylistic analysis ruled out a modern work.`,source:`James Cahill, The Case Against The Riverbank: An Indictment in Fourteen Counts (1999), jamescahill.info; The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry for the scroll (2016.750)`,url:`https://jamescahill.info/the-writings-of-james-cahill/cahill-lectures-and-papers/45-clp-clp-35-1999-qthe-case-against-the-riverbank-an-indictment-in-fourteen-countsq-symposium-met-museum-nyc`},about:`The image is 220.3 cm high, the tallest of all surviving early Chinese landscape paintings, and the silk has darkened with age. Dong Yuan was a court official of the Southern Tang, whose capital was Nanjing; his southern style left out contour lines and used soft washes and rubbed strokes for the rounded hills of the southeast. An inscription reads Painted by the Assistant Administrator of the Rear Park, Servitor Dong, and the label strip was written by Zhang Daqian, who owned the scroll before the C. C. Wang family. The Met says the scroll was split in two along its vertical seam in the fourteenth century.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39542`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:109.2,heightCm:220.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39542`,ratio:.5182},{id:`utamaro-tree-cricket-firefly`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Tree Cricket and Firefly, from the Picture Book of Crawling Creatures`,artist:`Kitagawa Utamaro`,culture:`Edo period, Japan`,date:`1788`,medium:`Page from woodblock printed book; ink and color on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A tree cricket and a firefly among grasses, one of fifteen designs in Utamaro's 1788 picture book of insects and comic poems.`,line:{text:`In the book's afterword Utamaro's teacher Sekien recalled how closely he had studied small creatures as a child.`,context:`Toriyama Sekien, afterword to Ehon mushi erami, 1788.`,source:`Museum für Asiatische Kunst, Staatliche Museen zu Berlin, collection entry for its copy of Ehon mushi erami`,url:`https://smb.museum-digital.de/object/63361`},about:`The page comes from Ehon mushi erami, the Picture Book of Crawling Creatures, published in two volumes in 1788 by Tsutaya Jūzaburō with fifteen designs of insects and comic kyōka poems. The poet Yadoya no Meshimori chose the poems and wrote the preface, and several leading kyōka poets of the day contributed. Meshimori's preface points to a seventeenth-century illustrated book of insect poems that probably served Utamaro as a model. The quality of the printing shows how highly the publisher rated Utamaro's work.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37288`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:18.4,heightCm:26.7},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37288`,ratio:1.4372},{id:`sin-hak-gwon-inner-geumgang`,kind:`painting`,title:`General View of Inner Geumgang`,artist:`Sin Hak-gwon`,culture:`Joseon dynasty, Korea`,date:`mid-19th century`,medium:`Six sheets of paper mounted as a single panel; ink and light color on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`The rocky peaks, streams and temples of Korea's Diamond Mountains run from right to left across one long panorama.`,line:{text:`Sin Hak-gwon never went to the Diamond Mountains; he learned to paint them from Jeong Seon's pictures.`,context:`According to Met curator Soyoung Lee. In his writings he longed to go but felt that, at sixty, he never would.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Diamond Mountains: A Conversation with Curator Soyoung Lee`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/diamond-mountains-curator-conversation`},about:`Six sheets of paper are mounted as one panel 235 cm long, and the painting names forty-six famous sites in the mountains, read from right to left. Sin Hak-gwon followed the style of Jeong Seon (1676–1759), who founded true-view landscape by travelling to places in Korea and painting them as he saw them, but he drew his spindly peaks with double outlines. Koreans speak of Mount Geumgang as a mountain of twelve thousand peaks. Since the division of the peninsula in 1945 it has been in North Korea.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/748432`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:235,heightCm:47.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/748432`,ratio:4.8797}],i=[{id:`kota-master-radha-krishna-grove`,kind:`painting`,title:`Radha and Krishna Walk in a Flowering Grove`,artist:`The Kota Master`,culture:`Kota, Rajasthan, India`,date:`c. 1720`,medium:`Ink, opaque watercolor, and gold on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Krishna, blue-skinned, walks with Radha through a grove of flowering trees at sunset, their arms and scarves entwined.`,line:{text:`The back of the sheet carries a second painting, Krishna Fluting, added about 1750–75.`,context:`The front was painted at Kota about 1720.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry 2003.178a, b (object 65594), title, date and label text`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/65594`},about:`Krishna is a Hindu god, one of the forms in which Vishnu appears, and Radha is his favourite consort; painters used their love to stand for a worshipper’s love for god. Here the birds, the flowering trees and the twining of the lovers’ bodies and scarves all carry their feeling, an old idea in Indian poetry and painting that nature echoes human passion. At Kota and other Rajasthani courts, nobles liked to be shown in the roles of Krishna and Radha.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/65594`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:11.1,heightCm:19.1},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/65594`,ratio:.6999},{id:`gita-govinda-krishna-gopis-yamuna`,kind:`painting`,title:`Krishna and the Gopis on the Bank of the Yamuna`,artist:`Unknown artist`,culture:`Pahari, Kangra or Guler, India`,date:`c. 1775–80`,medium:`Opaque watercolor and gold on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Krishna and Radha embrace under a tree by a river, while the cowherd women around them reach out in longing.`,line:{text:`The series may have been made for the first wedding of Sansar Chand of Kangra in 1781, when he was sixteen.`,context:`Painted in the Punjab Hills about 1775–80 by descendants of the painters Manaku and Nainsukh.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry 2017.736 (object 765654), label text`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/765654`},about:`The Gita Govinda, or Song of the Cowherd, is a poem written by Jayadeva in the twelfth century about the love of Krishna and Radha, spiritual and physical at once. In this folio the couple embrace under a tree by the Yamuna while the gopis around them reach out in longing. The painters were descendants of the brothers Manaku and Nainsukh, and the calm countryside and gentle figures are marks of Nainsukh’s family style. The series opens not with the lovers but with the god Indra, his body covered in eyes, asking the elephant-headed Ganesha to remove obstacles.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/765654`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:25.7,heightCm:16.2},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/765654`,ratio:1.5322},{id:`mandi-bhairavi-ragini`,kind:`painting`,title:`Bhairavi Ragini, from a Ragamala`,artist:`Unknown artist`,culture:`Mandi, Himachal Pradesh, India`,date:`c. 1640–50`,medium:`Opaque watercolor, silver, gold and ink on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A woman worships at a white shrine to the god Shiva inside a palace compound, beside a lotus pond.`,line:{text:`The page paints a musical mode: the verse above says she sings hymns to Shiva with hand cymbals.`,context:`A folio from a ragamala, a garland of ragas, painted at Mandi about 1640–50.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry 58.1.1 (object 37857), label text; title gives the ragamala`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37857`},about:`A ragamala is a set of paintings that evoke the moods of ragas, each tied to a season or a time of day; the word raga comes from a Sanskrit word for colour. Bhairavi is the wife of Bhairava, a god who is also a lord of music, and she gives her name to this mode and to the woman who embodies it. Inside the white shrine is a linga, the emblem of Shiva, on which she has laid fresh flowers. The strong, glowing colours point to the page’s real subject, the passion of devotion, called bhakti.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37857`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:16.5,heightCm:22.9},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37857`,ratio:.7625},{id:`payag-shah-jahan-on-horseback`,kind:`painting`,title:`Shah Jahan on Horseback`,artist:`Payag`,culture:`Mughal, India`,date:`c. 1630`,medium:`Ink, opaque watercolor, and gold on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`The Mughal emperor Shah Jahan, with a halo, rides a white and dark horse in rich trappings, holding a spear.`,line:{text:`Shah Jahan wrote the painter’s name in the border himself; a tiny signature was later found on the gold tip of the bow.`,context:`A folio from the Shah Jahan Album, painted about 1630.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry 55.121.10.21 (object 451267), label text`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451267`},about:`Payag worked for three emperors, Akbar, Jahangir and Shah Jahan, over a career of about seventy years; his brother Balchand was also a painter. The page comes from an album begun by Jahangir and added to by his son Shah Jahan. Its fifty folios are now split between the Met, which has forty-one, and the Freer Gallery of Art in Washington, which has nine. Other portraits in the album are frank, such as the aged Akbar with his fleshy neck; this one is idealised, the emperor richly dressed, haloed and mounted on an imperial stallion.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451267`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:25.7,heightCm:38.9},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451267`,ratio:.6673},{id:`ravi-varma-there-comes-papa`,kind:`painting`,title:`There Comes Papa`,artist:`Raja Ravi Varma`,culture:`India`,date:`1893`,medium:`Oil on canvas`,place:`Private collection`,caption:`A mother dressed in white holds her small son and points out his father's arrival, while their pet dog looks the same way.`,line:{text:`Ravi Varma painted it from a photograph of his daughter Mahaprabha holding her son; it went to the Chicago World’s Fair in 1893.`,context:`Mahaprabha (1872–1919) was his eldest daughter; the boy is her son Marthanda Varma.`,source:`The Ganesh Shivaswamy Foundation, entry for the painting on Google Arts & Culture, description text`,url:`https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/there-comes-papa-raja-ravi-varma/_AFv7N8y_RbNbg`},about:`A woman from an aristocratic Malabar family holds her small son and points out his father’s arrival, and the family dog looks the same way. It was one of ten paintings Ravi Varma sent to the World’s Columbian Exposition, and it is still in its original frame, with the notation it carried there. Ravi Varma painted royalty and Hindu myths in an academic realist style, and demand for copies led to the Ravi Varma Fine Art Lithographic Press in Bombay, which began printing in 1894 and made his pictures widely available.`,learnMore:{label:`Ganesh Shivaswamy Foundation`,url:`https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/there-comes-papa-raja-ravi-varma/_AFv7N8y_RbNbg`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:81,heightCm:125},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`The Ganesh Shivaswamy Foundation via Google Arts & Culture, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Ravi_Varma,_There_Comes_Papa_(1893).jpg`,ratio:.6391},{id:`tibet-mandala-of-jnanadakini`,kind:`painting`,title:`Mandala of Jnanadakini`,artist:`Unknown artist`,culture:`Sakya school, Tibet`,date:`late 14th century`,medium:`Distemper on cloth`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A square sacred diagram inside rings of colour, with the goddess Jnanadakini at the centre and teachers and deities around the edge.`,line:{text:`Probably one of a set of forty-two mandalas; the fine detail suggests a travelling Newari painter from Nepal made it in Tibet.`,context:`Painted in the late 14th century for the Sakya school; the set followed ritual texts called the Vajravali.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry 1987.16 (object 37802), label text`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37802`},about:`In Tibet a mandala is a diagram of the universe, a map of true reality, used as an aid to meditation on a rapid path to enlightenment. At the centre is the six-armed goddess Jnanadakini, ringed by eight forms of herself in the colours of the four quarters, with four guardian goddesses in the gateways. The outer circles hold lotus petals, vajras, flames and the eight great burial grounds. In the bottom row, among protective deities, a monk performs a consecration ritual.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37802`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:83.8,heightCm:74.9},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37802`,ratio:.8463},{id:`ravi-varma-press-lakshmi`,kind:`print`,title:`Lakshmi`,artist:`Ravi Varma Press, after Raja Ravi Varma`,culture:`Bombay (now Mumbai), India`,date:`1894`,medium:`Chromolithograph on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Lakshmi, goddess of wealth, stands on a lotus in a colour print based on Raja Ravi Varma's oil paintings of her.`,line:{text:`Printed in 1894, the first year of the Ravi Varma Press. Much simpler versions of it were still being printed into the 1920s.`,context:`Every colour needed its own lithographic stone, and this print uses many.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry 2013.10 (object 78264), label text`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/78264`},about:`It may be the best-known early lithograph of a Hindu god. Ravi Varma based it on a series of oil paintings of Lakshmi made for different patrons, one of which is in the Maharaja Fatesingh Museum in Vadodara. The scholar Mark Barron dates it to 1894 from its oversized sheet, uneven paper, brush-edge stippling and lack of varnish. The initials A.S.D. on the base stand for Anant Shivaji Desai, one of the first two distributors of the press’s prints, and much simpler versions were still being printed into the 1920s.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/78264`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:50.8,heightCm:71.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/78264`,ratio:.6726},{id:`basohli-kalki-avatar`,kind:`painting`,title:`Kalki Avatar, the Future Incarnation of Vishnu`,artist:`Unknown artist`,culture:`Pahari, Basohli, India`,date:`c. 1700–1710`,medium:`Opaque watercolor, gold, silver, and beetle-wing cases on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Vishnu, a blue-skinned warrior, is visited by Kalki, his own future form, who comes as a groom leading a white horse.`,line:{text:`The green gems on the armour and bridle are pieces of real beetle wing cases; the two on the bridle are under a millimetre across.`,context:`Found by Met conservators in a 2025 study of beetle-wing inlay; they are the smallest pieces in the collection.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Perspectives, “Painted with Beetles” by Olivia Dill and Marina Ruiz Molina (2025)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/painted-with-beetles`},about:`Vishnu sits as a blue-skinned warrior while Kalki, his own future form, arrives as a groom leading a white horse. The shimmer of the gems is not pigment: it comes from light reflecting off very thin layers in the beetle’s shell, and painters thinned the wing cases, cut them to shape and glued them on in the last stages of the work. The gem on Vishnu’s silver helmet shows blue and purple, so it may stand for a sapphire or an amethyst. The beetle wings, the patterned saddle blanket, the throne legs and the almond-shaped eyes all point to Basohli, but the page cannot be matched to any known set of Vishnu’s avatars.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37889`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:26.7,heightCm:17.1},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37889`,ratio:1.4472},{id:`nepal-vasudhara-mandala-1777`,kind:`painting`,title:`Vasudhara Mandala`,artist:`Unknown artist`,culture:`Nepal`,date:`dated 1777`,medium:`Distemper on cloth`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Vasudhara, Buddhist goddess of wealth and abundance, sits at the centre of a mandala dated 1777 (Samvat 897).`,line:{text:`Dated Samvat 897, which is 1777. Newar Buddhists still offer Vasudhara mandalas at her yearly harvest festival.`,context:`The festival falls on a full moon in the lunar month of Bhadra.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, collection entry 2015.299 (object 682119), date field; Los Angeles County Museum of Art, entry for Mandala of Vasundhara, dated 1495 (M.77.19.7), curatorial notes`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/682119`},about:`Vasudhara’s name means Stream of Gems. She is a goddess of wealth and abundance, and also of learning and the whole of Mahayana Buddhist wisdom, shown by the book in her uppermost left hand. The bodhisattvas Avalokiteshvara, in red, and Vajrapani, in green, sit on either side of her, and below her is the golden figure of Ila Devi, who mediates between the goddess and her devotees. Four yakshas, protective nature deities, guard the gates, and other yakshas around the mandala pour gems from sacks and open treasure chests.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/682119`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:72.4,heightCm:85.7},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/682119`,ratio:.8386},{id:`isarda-bhagavata-purana-gopis`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Gopis Plead with Krishna to Return Their Clothing, folio from the “Isarda” Bhagavata Purana`,artist:`Master of the “Isarda” Bhagavata Purana`,culture:`North India, Delhi–Agra area`,date:`c. 1560–65`,year:1560,medium:`Opaque watercolor and ink on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Krishna sits in a tree with the cowherd women’s clothes while the women stand in a dark river below, hands raised.`,line:{text:`From a Bhagavata Purana of about 1560 that the Met calls perhaps the greatest of the pre-Mughal Hindu manuscripts known.`,context:`The river runs off the edge of the page, past the painting’s own frame.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entry 1972.260 (object 37962), overview text (Wayback copy of 16 September 2025)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37962`},about:`The Bhagavata Purana tells the life of Krishna. Here the gopis, the cowherd women of Vrindavan, have left their clothes on the bank to bathe; Krishna has taken them up into a tree, and the women stand in the water praying to him, which the text treats as an image of the soul’s longing for god. The series is named after Isarda, the Rajasthani estate where it was kept, and was painted a few years before the Mughal style took hold in north India. The Met points out that the river runs unchecked off the page, defying the frame.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37962`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:25.7,heightCm:18.7},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37962`,ratio:1.3413},{id:`guler-durga-forms-of-mahishasura`,kind:`painting`,title:`Durga Attacking the Buffalo, Lion, Human, and Elephant Forms of Mahishasura, folio from a Devimahatmya`,artist:`Unknown painter`,culture:`Pahari, Guler, Himachal Pradesh`,date:`c. 1770–1780`,year:1770,medium:`Opaque watercolor and gold on paper`,place:`Los Angeles County Museum of Art`,caption:`The many-armed goddess on her lion fights a buffalo, a lion, an armed man and an elephant on a pale ground inside an orange border.`,line:{text:`The demon changes shape as he fights: buffalo, lion, warrior, elephant. The painter put all four into one picture.`,context:`When the goddess cut off the elephant’s trunk, he became a buffalo again, and she killed him.`,source:`Los Angeles County Museum of Art, object record M.74.14, curatorial notes (fetched 22 October 2026)`,url:`https://collections.lacma.org/object/33458`},about:`The Devimahatmya, about 700 Sanskrit verses, presents the Goddess as the supreme power of the universe and tells of the demons she defeats. The page shows the end of its third chapter, the slaying of Mahishasura, whose name means buffalo demon. Other pages from the same set are in the Brooklyn Museum and the Morgan Library in New York. The image is 17 by 27 cm, and the pale ground and thin orange border are typical of Guler work of the 1770s.`,learnMore:{label:`LACMA`,url:`https://collections.lacma.org/object/33458`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:26.67,heightCm:16.83},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Los Angeles County Museum of Art, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://collections.lacma.org/object/33458`,ratio:1.4496},{id:`guler-hanuman-mountain-of-herbs`,kind:`painting`,title:`Hanuman Revives Rama and Lakshmana with Medicinal Herbs, folio from a dispersed Ramayana series`,artist:`Guler workshop`,culture:`Pahari, Punjab Hills, Guler`,date:`c. 1790`,year:1790,medium:`Ink and opaque watercolor on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A green hillside strewn with wounded monkeys and bears; Hanuman flies in at upper left with a mountain peak, and Rama and Lakshmana lie among their army.`,line:{text:`Sent for one healing herb, Hanuman could not tell which it was, so he broke off the whole mountain peak and carried it back.`,context:`He flies in at the upper left. Below, Rama and Lakshmana lie wounded among their army of monkeys and bears.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entries 1987.424.13 (object 37988) and 1994.310 (object 37960), overview text as quoted in search results (pages not loaded directly; marked partly verified); the episode is in Valmiki’s Ramayana, Yuddha Kanda`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37988`},about:`In the war for Lanka, Rama and his brother Lakshmana fall unconscious, and only herbs from a mountain in the Himalaya can save them. The painters of the Punjab Hills told the Ramayana in long sets of pages like this one, and the Met notes how this workshop carried on the Kangra manner, with close natural detail and a landscape that recedes into distance. The pale hill, the scattered bodies and the small flying figure make the scale of the rescue clear. The page is 25 by 35 cm.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37988`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:35.4,heightCm:25.2},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/37988`,ratio:1.3808}],a=[{id:`habiballah-concourse-of-the-birds`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Concourse of the Birds`,artist:`Habiballah of Sava`,culture:`Safavid, Isfahan, Iran`,date:`c. 1600`,medium:`Ink, opaque watercolor, gold, and silver on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Birds of many kinds gather in a landscape to hear the hoopoe, who will lead them on a spiritual journey in Attar's poem.`,line:{text:`The hunter with a musket at upper right has no place in the poem. His gun, used in Iran only after the mid-1500s, dates the painting.`,context:`The text was copied in Herat in the 1480s; this picture was added in Isfahan about 1600.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entry (overview) for 63.210.11`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451725`},about:`Farid al-Din ʿAttar wrote the Language of the Birds about 1187. The birds stand for human souls; the hoopoe, on a rock at the right, urges them to seek the Simurgh, their true king, through seven valleys. The manuscript was copied in Herat in the 1480s with some pictures left unpainted, and about 1600 it was refurbished in Isfahan, probably for Shah ʿAbbas I, when this painting was added. Habiballah signed it on a small rock between the duck and the geese.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451725`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:11.4,heightCm:25.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451725`,ratio:.6618},{id:`al-jazari-elephant-clock`,kind:`illustration`,title:`The Elephant Clock`,artist:`After al-Jazari`,culture:`Mamluk, probably Syria or Iraq`,date:`1315`,medium:`Ink, opaque watercolor, and gold on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A design for an automated clock built on the back of an elephant, from a 1315 copy of al-Jazari's book of machines.`,line:{text:`Every half hour the bird on the dome whistled, a ball dropped into the dragon’s mouth, and the driver struck the elephant with his goad.`,context:`From a copy of al-Jazari’s book of machines made in 1315.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entry (overview) for 57.51.23`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451402`},about:`Al-Jazari wrote his book of machines in the early 13th century for Nasir al-Din Mahmud, ruler of Amid, now Diyarbakır. It describes fifty devices, among them clocks, fountains, drinking vessels and machines for hand-washing and bloodletting; the elephant clock has a chapter of its own, with instructions for building it. Farrukh ibn ʿAbd al-Latif copied this manuscript in 1315. Complete, it had 150 folios with nine diagrams and 98 paintings, and few illustrated manuscripts of this date are known from Syria; its pages are now spread among collections.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451402`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:19.7,heightCm:30},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/451402`,ratio:.7013}],o=[{id:`indus-unicorn-seal`,kind:`objects`,title:`Stamp seal and modern impression: unicorn and incense burner (?)`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Indus, Mature Harappan`,date:`c. 2600–1900 BCE`,year:-2600,medium:`Burnt steatite`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A small square Indus seal with a one-horned animal and a line of undeciphered script, beside its modern clay impression.`,line:{text:`The signs along the top are Indus script, in use from about 2600 BCE and still undeciphered. Most inscriptions run to about five signs.`,context:`The seal was pressed into wet clay to mark goods; the impression beside it is modern.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entry 49.40.1 (object 324062), overview text (Wayback copy); Wikipedia, "Indus script" (average length about five signs)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324062`},about:`Hundreds of square stamp seals like this were found in the cities of the Indus civilisation. They were cut from soft steatite and then fired to harden them, and served as marks of ownership and badges of status. The animal is shown in strict profile before an object that may be an altar or incense burner, with a decorated cover over its shoulder; it is usually called a unicorn, but is more likely a bull drawn with one horn hiding the other. John Marshall, who led the Indus excavations in the 1920s, acquired this seal in the Punjab, and the Met bought it from him in 1949.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324062`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:3.8,heightCm:3.8},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324062`,ratio:1.0035},{id:`mohenjo-daro-dancing-girl`,kind:`objects`,title:`Dancing Girl`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Indus civilisation, Mohenjo-daro`,date:`c. 2300–1750 BCE`,year:-2300,medium:`Bronze, lost-wax cast`,place:`National Museum, New Delhi`,caption:`A small bronze figure of a naked girl standing with one hand on her hip and a stack of bangles on her left arm.`,line:{text:`Dug up at Mohenjo-daro in 1926 and cast by the lost-wax method about 4,000 years ago. It is 10.5 cm tall.`,context:`The name was John Marshall’s; the pose reminded him of dancers of his own day.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Dancing Girl (prehistoric sculpture)" (Mackay excavation 1926; 10.5 cm; lost-wax, citing McIntosh 2008; Marshall’s naming); Wikipedia, "Mohenjo-daro"`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dancing_Girl_(prehistoric_sculpture)`},about:`Ernest Mackay found the figure in the HR area of Mohenjo-daro, in what is now Sindh, Pakistan. Her left arm is covered with bangles from wrist to shoulder and she stands with her weight on one leg, her right hand on her hip. Lost-wax casting means the maker modelled her in wax, coated it in clay, melted the wax out and poured bronze into the hollow, which is why a figure this small has such free limbs. At Partition in 1947 she was allotted to India, and she is now in the National Museum in New Delhi.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dancing_Girl_(prehistoric_sculpture)`},licence:`cc0`,credit:`Photo: Gary Todd, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_%22Dancing_Girl,%22_Mohenjo-daro,_c._2500_BC.jpg`,ratio:.9523},{id:`didarganj-yakshi`,kind:`objects`,title:`Didarganj Yakshi (Chauri bearer)`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Mauryan or Kushan, Pataliputra`,date:`c. 3rd century BCE or 2nd century CE`,year:-250,medium:`Chunar sandstone, polished`,place:`Bihar Museum, Patna`,caption:`A life-size standing woman in polished sandstone holding a fly whisk over her shoulder, with heavy jewellery and a knotted lower garment.`,line:{text:`Dug out of the bank of the Ganges at Didarganj, Patna, on 18 October 1917. Its makers polished the stone to a mirror finish.`,context:`The polish is typical of Mauryan work of the 3rd century BCE, but the figure may be some centuries later.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Didarganj Yakshi" (found 18 October 1917 by villagers; Chunar sandstone with mirror-like polish; dating debate, 3rd century BCE to 2nd century CE)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Didarganj_Yakshi`},about:`She is 1.57 m tall on a pedestal of about half a metre, carved from a single block of buff sandstone from Chunar. In her right hand she holds a chauri, the fly whisk carried by attendants of kings and gods, so she is read as a yakshi, a nature spirit, or as a royal attendant. The polish is the kind found on pillars of the emperor Ashoka, which is why she was long dated to his century; the way the hair and jewellery are cut has led others to date her to the Kushan period, about 400 years later. She stood in the Patna Museum for a century and is now in the Bihar Museum nearby.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Didarganj_Yakshi`},licence:`cc-by-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Anandajoti Bhikkhu, CC BY 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Didarganj-Yakshi-3bc-Patna.jpg`,ratio:.3581},{id:`udayagiri-varaha`,kind:`objects`,title:`Vishnu as Varaha rescuing the Earth, Cave 5`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Gupta period, Udayagiri, Madhya Pradesh`,date:`c. 401–412`,year:401,medium:`Rock-cut sandstone relief`,place:`Udayagiri Caves, Vidisha, India`,caption:`A rock-cut relief of the boar-headed god Varaha standing in a cave mouth with the earth goddess on his shoulder, watched by rows of small figures.`,line:{text:`An inscription in the cave next door names King Chandragupta II and the Gupta year 82, which is about 401 CE.`,context:`The Varaha panel itself may have been cut a decade later.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Udayagiri Caves" (Cave 6 inscription, Chandragupta II, Gupta year 82 = c. 401 CE; Stietencron’s date of 410–412 for the panel; Willis quoted)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Udayagiri_Caves`},about:`Varaha is Vishnu in the form of a boar, who dived into the cosmic ocean to lift the drowned earth, shown as the goddess Bhudevi clinging to his shoulder. He fills the open front of a small cave, far larger than the rows of gods and sages who watch him; the king and his minister are thought to be among the figures. The art historian Michael Willis calls the panel the iconographic centre-piece of Udayagiri, and the caves are read as a statement of the Gupta king’s power. They are among the earliest dated Hindu monuments.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Udayagiri_Caves`},licence:`cc-by-sa-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Asitjain, CC BY-SA 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varahavtar_Panel.jpg`,ratio:1.4938},{id:`gupta-krishna-killing-keshi`,kind:`objects`,title:`Krishna Killing the Horse Demon Keshi`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Gupta period, Uttar Pradesh`,date:`5th century`,year:450,medium:`Terracotta`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A terracotta relief of the young Krishna forcing his elbow into the mouth of a rearing horse demon while holding it down with his foot.`,line:{text:`Temple building began in earnest in India in the Gupta period. Brick and stone temples carried terracotta plaques like this one.`,context:`Krishna was a favourite subject for them.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline entry for 1991.300 (Wayback copy of 30 November 2024)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/38511`},about:`Krishna, a form of the god Vishnu, kills the demon Keshi, who has taken the shape of a horse. He pins the animal with his foot and drives his elbow down its throat; below them lie the dead horse and the dung it let out at the moment of death. The Met suggests the subject goes back to Greek stories of Hercules and the man-eating horses of Diomedes, carried to India after Alexander. The plaque is 53 cm high and was made to be set into the wall of a brick temple.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/38511`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:40.6,heightCm:53.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/38511`,ratio:.8001},{id:`ajanta-padmapani`,kind:`painting`,title:`Bodhisattva Padmapani, Cave 1`,artist:`Unknown painters`,culture:`Vakataka period, Ajanta, Maharashtra`,date:`c. 475–500`,year:475,medium:`Pigment on dry plaster over rock`,place:`Ajanta Caves, Aurangabad district, India`,caption:`A mural of a bodhisattva with a tall crown, eyes lowered, holding a blue lotus, surrounded by smaller figures and animals.`,line:{text:`On 28 April 1819 a British officer out hunting tigers was shown the caves by a shepherd boy. He scratched his name over a painting.`,context:`John Smith’s name and the date are still on a wall of Cave 10. Local people had never lost the caves.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Ajanta Caves" (John Smith, 28th Cavalry, 28 April 1819, shown Cave 10 by a shepherd boy; scratched his name over a bodhisattva; Cave 1 commissioned by Harishena per Walter Spink; painted on dry plaster)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajanta_Caves`},about:`Padmapani, the lotus bearer, is a form of Avalokiteshvara, the bodhisattva of compassion. He stands over life size beside the door to the Buddha shrine at the back of Cave 1, paired with Vajrapani on the other side. The caves were cut into a cliff above a river bend, and Cave 1 was paid for by the Vakataka king Harishena in the last years of the fifth century. The painters worked on a dry plaster ground, not wet fresco, which is why so much has flaked away and why what remains is so delicate.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajanta_Caves`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Photograph reproduced from Le Musée absolu (Phaidon, 2012), via Wikimedia Commons`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodhisattva_Padmapani,_cave_1,_Ajanta,_India.jpg`,ratio:.8541},{id:`elephanta-trimurti`,kind:`objects`,title:`Trimurti (Sadashiva), Cave 1`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Kalachuri or early Chalukya, Elephanta Island`,date:`c. 6th century`,year:550,medium:`Rock-cut basalt`,place:`Elephanta Caves, Mumbai Harbour, India`,caption:`Three huge carved faces of Shiva, calm in the middle, fierce and gentle at the sides, filling a deep niche in a rock-cut cave.`,line:{text:`The Portuguese named the island after a stone elephant that stood near the landing. The elephant is now in a Mumbai garden.`,context:`The caves were cut about the 6th century; much of their carving was damaged in the Portuguese period.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Elephanta Caves" (name from the stone elephant; moved to Victoria Gardens in 1864, reassembled 1914, now in Jijamata Udyaan; date c. 5th–6th century, completed by about 550)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephanta_Caves`},about:`The three heads are one god. The central face is Shiva at peace, the right face scowls with a curled moustache and a serpent, and the left face is soft and holds a lotus; together they are read as creation, protection and destruction, or as the whole of Shiva beyond his separate forms. The carving is about six metres high and faces the main entrance of the great cave, so a visitor walks towards it down the length of the hall. The island is an hour by boat from the Gateway of India, and the caves were listed by UNESCO in 1987.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephanta_Caves`},licence:`cc-by-2.0`,credit:`Photo: François Fougère, CC BY 2.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sadashiva_Trimurti_Pancamukha_Shiva_Elephanta_island.jpg`,ratio:.6642},{id:`chola-shiva-nataraja`,kind:`objects`,title:`Shiva as Lord of Dance (Nataraja)`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Chola period, Tamil Nadu`,date:`c. late 11th century`,year:1070,medium:`Copper alloy`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A bronze of the four-armed Shiva dancing on one leg inside a ring of flames, his hair flying out, a dwarf crushed under his foot.`,line:{text:`Cast by the lost-wax method in the late 11th century, when Chola temples carried bronzes like this one through the streets in procession.`,context:`The lugs on the base took the carrying poles.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entry 1987.80.1 (object 39328), date and overview (Wayback copy); Wikipedia, "Nataraja" (Chola bronzes made by lost-wax casting; part of Chola-era processions and festivals)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39328`},about:`The Met calls the image a brilliant invention: one figure holds Shiva’s roles as creator, keeper and destroyer of the universe. The drum in his upper right hand made the first sounds of creation; the fire in his upper left will end it; the lower right hand says do not fear, and the lower left points to the raised foot, a sign of refuge. The dwarf under his foot is illusion. The ring of flames is the cosmos, and his hair flies out with the force of the dance.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39328`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:56.5,heightCm:68.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39328`,ratio:.7501},{id:`pala-vishnu-lakshmi-sarasvati`,kind:`objects`,title:`Vishnu Accompanied by Lakshmi and Sarasvati`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Pala period, Bangladesh`,date:`12th century`,year:1150,medium:`Black phyllite`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A tall black stone stele of Vishnu standing crowned between two smaller goddesses, with attendants and a pointed frame of carved ornament.`,line:{text:`In a convention found only in medieval Bengal, Vishnu stands with Lakshmi and Sarasvati, not his wives Sri Devi and Bhu Devi.`,context:`The stone is 1.2 m high and was made to stand in a temple niche.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entry 1991.406 (object 38530), overview text as quoted in search results (page itself not loaded; marked partly verified)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/38530`},about:`Vishnu holds his mace, discus and conch and wears a tall crown; Lakshmi, on his right, carries a fly whisk and a lotus, and Sarasvati, on his left, plays the vina. The dark, fine-grained stone was carved so crisply that the ornament of the frame still reads like metalwork. Steles of this kind were made in great numbers in Bengal under the Pala and Sena kings, when the region was the last great centre of Buddhist and Hindu sculpture in eastern India. This one is 119 cm high.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/38530`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:63.5,heightCm:119.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/38530`,ratio:.7503}],s=[{id:`tell-asmar-standing-worshiper`,kind:`objects`,title:`Standing male worshiper`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Sumerian, Early Dynastic I–II, Eshnunna (Tell Asmar)`,date:`c. 2900–2600 BCE`,year:-2900,medium:`Gypsum alabaster, shell, black limestone, bitumen`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A small pale stone figure of a bearded man with enormous inlaid eyes, hands clasped at his chest, in a fringed skirt.`,line:{text:`It stood in a temple at Tell Asmar to pray perpetually on behalf of the person it represented.`,context:`Found in 1933 buried beneath the temple floor with eleven others.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline entry for 40.156 (Wayback copy of 9 January 2025); Wikipedia, "Tell Asmar Hoard" (twelve statues found 1933–34 under the floor of the Abu Temple by Henri Frankfort’s Oriental Institute expedition)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/323735`},about:`In Mesopotamia a statue was not a likeness but a stand-in: the worshiper was present in it, and the god in his. The eyes are shell with pupils of black limestone set in bitumen, and their size gives the figure its fixed attention. Henri Frankfort’s expedition found twelve such figures, ten men and two women, packed together under the floor of the Square Temple, where old statues had been retired. This one is 29.5 cm high.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/323735`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:12.9,heightCm:29.5},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/323735`,ratio:.75},{id:`standard-of-ur-peace`,kind:`objects`,title:`The Standard of Ur, “Peace” side`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Sumerian, Early Dynastic III, Ur`,date:`c. 2550 BCE`,year:-2550,medium:`Shell, red limestone and lapis lazuli set in bitumen on wood`,place:`The British Museum, London`,caption:`Three rows of small figures in shell on a blue ground: a seated king and feasting guests above, men leading animals and carrying goods below.`,line:{text:`Found crushed in a plundered tomb at Ur in 1927–28. Woolley guessed it was a standard carried on a pole; no one knows what it was.`,context:`The wood had rotted and the soil had flattened it; the present box shape is a reconstruction.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Standard of Ur" (Woolley, Royal Cemetery, tomb PG 779, 1927–28; fragmentary, crushed; the name and Woolley’s guess not confirmed; 21.59 × 49.53 cm)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_of_Ur`},about:`Leonard Woolley’s team found it in the corner of a tomb chamber in the Royal Cemetery at Ur, near the shoulder of a man. The two long sides are read as war and peace: the other side shows chariots, soldiers and prisoners, and this one a banquet with a lyre player and a procession of men bringing fish, sheep and goods, perhaps the feast after the victory. The figures are cut from shell and set in bitumen against a ground of lapis lazuli, a stone that reached Ur from Afghanistan. It is 49.5 cm long and 21.6 cm high.`,learnMore:{label:`The British Museum`,url:`https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/W_1928-1010-3`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:49.53,heightCm:21.59},licence:`cc-by-sa-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Geni, corrected by LeastCommonAncestor, CC BY-SA 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_Ur_-_Peace.jpg`,ratio:2.1833},{id:`hammurabi-stele-relief`,kind:`objects`,title:`Code of Hammurabi, the relief at the top of the stele`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Old Babylonian, Babylon`,date:`c. 1755–1750 BCE`,year:-1755,medium:`Basalt`,place:`Musée du Louvre, Paris`,caption:`The top of a dark basalt stele: King Hammurabi stands with his hand raised before the seated sun god Shamash, who holds out a rod and ring.`,line:{text:`Law 196 reads: If an awīlu should blind the eye of another awīlu, they shall blind his eye. An awīlu was a free man.`,context:`In Martha Roth’s translation. The relief shows Hammurabi receiving his authority from Shamash, god of the sun and of justice.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Code of Hammurabi", quoting Martha T. Roth, Law Collections from Mesopotamia and Asia Minor (1997); Louvre collection record Sb 8 (basalt, 225 × 79 × 47 cm, found at Susa 1901–02)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_of_Hammurabi`},about:`Below the relief, some 4,130 lines of cuneiform set out the laws of Hammurabi, king of Babylon, numbered by modern editors up to 282. The stele is 2.25 m high and was carried off to Susa, in what is now Iran, as plunder by an Elamite king; Jacques de Morgan’s French expedition found it there in pieces between December 1901 and January 1902. Jean-Vincent Scheil published the first translation the same year. It is the longest and best preserved law text from the ancient Near East.`,learnMore:{label:`Musée du Louvre`,url:`https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436`},licence:`cc-by-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Mbzt, CC BY 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F0182_Louvre_Code_Hammourabi_Bas-relief_Sb8_rwk.jpg`,ratio:.9057},{id:`nimrud-lamassu`,kind:`objects`,title:`Human-headed winged lion (lamassu)`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Neo-Assyrian, Nimrud (ancient Kalhu)`,date:`c. 883–859 BCE`,year:-883,medium:`Gypsum alabaster`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Two huge stone guardians with bearded human heads in horned caps and winged animal bodies stand either side of a doorway in the Met’s Assyrian gallery.`,line:{text:`It has five legs, so it stands firm when seen from the front and strides forward when seen from the side.`,context:`It guarded a doorway in the palace of Ashurnasirpal II at Nimrud. It weighs about seven tonnes.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline entry for 32.143.2 (Wayback copy of 23 January 2025); Met API record (311.2 × 62.2 × 276.9 cm, 7,257 kg)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/322609`},about:`Ashurnasirpal II made Nimrud his capital and in 879 BCE held a ten-day feast for 69,574 people to celebrate it; his inscription says he set beasts of the mountains and the seas, fashioned from white limestone and alabaster, in the gates of the palace, and this is one of them. The horned cap marks it as divine and the belt as powerful. The cuneiform text cut across its body repeats the king’s account of his palace. The figure is 3.1 m high and came to New York as a gift of John D. Rockefeller Jr. in 1932.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/322609`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:276.9,heightCm:311.2},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/322609`,ratio:1.4075},{id:`ashurbanipal-lion-hunt`,kind:`objects`,title:`Ashurbanipal hunting lions on horseback, relief from the North Palace at Nineveh`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Neo-Assyrian, Nineveh`,date:`c. 645–635 BCE`,year:-645,medium:`Gypsum alabaster`,place:`The British Museum, London`,caption:`A low relief of the king on a galloping horse driving a spear into a lion that leaps at him, with a second lion fallen behind.`,line:{text:`The lions were caught beforehand and released from cages in an arena for the king to kill with arrows, spears or his sword.`,context:`Hormuzd Rassam dug the reliefs out of the North Palace at Nineveh in 1852–54.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Lion Hunt of Ashurbanipal" (lions released from cages; North Palace; excavated by Rassam 1852–1854; gypsum alabaster; Room 10a)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_Hunt_of_Ashurbanipal`},about:`The hunt was a royal rite: the king of Assyria proved his fitness to rule by killing the animal that stood for chaos. The reliefs lined a corridor of Ashurbanipal’s palace and show the whole event, from lions let out of their cages to the king pouring a libation over the dead. Julian Reade calls them the supreme masterpieces of Assyrian art, and the wounded and dying animals are carved with more feeling than any of the people. Rassam shipped the slabs to London, where they fill a room of the British Museum.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_Hunt_of_Ashurbanipal`},licence:`cc-by-sa-4.0`,credit:`Photo: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Assyrian_king_Ashurbanipal_on_his_horse_thrusting_a_spear_onto_a_lion%E2%80%99s_head._Alabaster_bas-relief_from_Nineveh,_dating_back_to_645-635_BCE_and_is_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum,_London.jpg`,ratio:1.5886},{id:`babylon-striding-lion`,kind:`objects`,title:`Panel with striding lion`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Neo-Babylonian, Babylon`,date:`c. 604–562 BCE`,year:-604,medium:`Glazed ceramic brick`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A lion in white and yellow glazed brick striding to the left against a deep blue ground.`,line:{text:`Stone is rare in southern Mesopotamia, so Babylon was built of moulded, glazed brick and became a city of brilliant colour.`,context:`Lions like this lined the Processional Way north of the Ishtar Gate, built by Nebuchadnezzar II.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline entry for 31.13.1 (Wayback copy of 5 January 2020); Wikipedia, "Ishtar Gate" (about 120 lions; bricks moulded, fired, glazed and fired again; Koldewey excavation)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/322585`},about:`The lion was the animal of Ishtar, goddess of love and war, and the Met says the repeated figures served to protect the street and to guide the New Year processions from the city to the temple. Each lion was made in pieces: the relief was pressed from clay in moulds, the bricks were fired, glazed in colour and fired again, then set with narrow joints sealed in bitumen. Robert Koldewey’s German expedition dug the gate and the street between 1899 and 1914, and most of the lions went to Berlin, where the gate is rebuilt in the Pergamon Museum. This panel is 97 by 227 cm.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/322585`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:227.3,heightCm:97.2},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/322585`,ratio:2.3069}],c=[{id:`olmec-colossal-head-2`,kind:`objects`,title:`Colossal Head 2 from San Lorenzo`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Olmec, San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán, Veracruz`,date:`c. 1200–900 BCE`,year:-1200,medium:`Basalt`,place:`Museo Nacional de Antropología, Mexico City`,caption:`A huge basalt head with a broad nose, full lips and a close-fitting helmet, pitted all over its surface.`,line:{text:`The basalt was moved more than 150 km from the Tuxtla mountains. This head is 2.69 m high and weighs about 20 tonnes.`,context:`Seventeen Olmec colossal heads are known; most are thought to be portraits of rulers.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Olmec colossal heads" (17 known; Cerro Cintepec basalt from the Sierra de los Tuxtlas moved over 150 km; San Lorenzo Head 2 in the Museo Nacional de Antropología, 2.69 m, about 20 tons; portraits of rulers)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olmec_colossal_heads`},about:`The Olmec were the first civilisation of Mesoamerica, and the heads are their best known work. Each was cut from a single boulder with stone tools, since the Olmec had no metal, and the boulders were brought by raft and by hauling from the volcanic hills to the north. The headgear differs from head to head, which is one reason they are read as portraits of particular rulers rather than gods. The first was found at Tres Zapotes in 1862; this one comes from San Lorenzo, the earliest Olmec centre.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olmec_colossal_heads`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:183,heightCm:269},licence:`cc0`,credit:`Photo: Gary Todd, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmec_Colossal_Head,_San_Lorenzo,_Veracruz,_1200-600_BC.jpg`,ratio:.6667},{id:`moche-portrait-head-bottle`,kind:`objects`,title:`Portrait Head Bottle`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Moche, north coast of Peru`,date:`5th–6th century`,year:450,medium:`Ceramic, slip, pigment`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A pottery bottle shaped as a man’s head with a painted headband and a tall stirrup-shaped spout rising from the top.`,line:{text:`Most were found in graves, but wear and old repairs show they were used in life. The faces seem to be of particular people.`,context:`The Moche made them in moulds, so one face could be repeated.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Moche portrait vessel" (retrieved mostly from graves but used in life, shown by wear marks and repairs; individualised faces); The Metropolitan Museum of Art, essay "Moche Portrait Head Vessels" as quoted in search results (called portraits for their resemblance to specific individuals)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308527`},about:`The Moche ruled the river valleys of Peru’s north coast from about 100 to 800 CE and left no writing, so their pottery is the fullest record of them. The portrait bottles are among the few realistic faces from the ancient Americas, and some men appear on several vessels at different ages. The stirrup spout, a loop with a single opening at the top, was used on this coast for some 2,500 years; it held liquid in and may have limited evaporation. This bottle is 32 cm high.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308527`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:18.4,heightCm:32.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308527`,ratio:.75},{id:`maya-vessel-chahk-and-jaguar`,kind:`objects`,title:`Vessel with mythological scene`,artist:`Metropolitan Painter`,culture:`Maya, Guatemala or Mexico`,date:`600–800 CE`,year:600,medium:`Ceramic, slip`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A small tripod cup painted in black line on cream, with a red rim, showing a jaguar figure on a mountain and the rain god with an axe.`,line:{text:`The glyphs name the rain god as Chahk of the First Rain, which places the scene at the start of the rains, in May or June.`,context:`Painted in the “codex style”, in the same thin black line as the surviving Maya books.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline entry for 1980.213 (Wayback copy of 5 January 2020)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/314217`},about:`A spotted jaguar in a white cape crouches on a living mountain while Chahk, the rain god, raises a great axe and a stone shaped like a skull, either attacking or dancing. The Met describes it as one of the best painted and best preserved mythological scenes on any Maya vessel, and the painter is known by name only from this and related cups. The scene tells a myth of the start of the rainy season, which decided the maize harvest. The cup is 11 cm high and stands on three stepped feet.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/314217`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:12.5,heightCm:11.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/314217`,ratio:.7398},{id:`pakal-jade-mask`,kind:`objects`,title:`Funerary mask of K’inich Janaab’ Pakal`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Maya, Palenque, Chiapas`,date:`683 CE`,year:683,medium:`Jade mosaic, shell, mother of pearl and obsidian`,place:`Museo Nacional de Antropología, Mexico City`,caption:`A life-size face made of hundreds of small green jade pieces, with eyes of white shell and black obsidian, shown with jade bead necklaces.`,line:{text:`Pakal ruled Palenque for 68 years and 33 days and died at 80. His tomb was found in 1952 under a temple stairway filled with rubble.`,context:`The mask lay on his face in a stone sarcophagus deep inside the Temple of the Inscriptions.`,source:`Wikipedia, "K’inich Janaab’ Pakal" (reign 615–683, 68 years and 33 days, died aged 80; stairway found by Alberto Ruz Lhuillier, tomb uncovered 1952); Wikipedia, "Temple of the Inscriptions" (mask of jade, eyes of shell, mother of pearl and obsidian)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%27inich_Janaab%27_Pakal`},about:`The Mexican archaeologist Alberto Ruz Lhuillier lifted a floor slab in the temple on top of the pyramid and found a stairway packed with rubble; it took four seasons to clear and led to a crypt at the base, the first royal tomb found inside a Maya pyramid. The king lay under a carved lid with this mask over his face and jade in his hands and mouth. Jade was the most precious material the Maya had, greener than gold, and the mosaic was fitted piece by piece on a backing that has since been renewed. The mask is in the national museum in Mexico City.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%27inich_Janaab%27_Pakal`},licence:`cc0`,credit:`Photo: Gary Todd, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maya_Jade_Mask_of_Kinich_Hanab_Pakal,_Ruler_of_Palenque,_615-683_AD.jpg`,ratio:1.5},{id:`yaxchilan-lintel-24`,kind:`objects`,title:`Lintel 24 from Structure 23, Yaxchilan`,artist:`Mo’ Chahk (named in the inscription)`,culture:`Maya, Yaxchilan, Chiapas`,date:`c. 709–726 CE`,year:709,medium:`Limestone with traces of paint`,place:`The British Museum, London`,caption:`A deep limestone relief of a kneeling queen pulling a thorned rope through her tongue while a king holds a flaming torch over her.`,line:{text:`The last glyphs name the carver, Mo’ Chahk, and the date of the rite: 5 Eb 15 Mak, which is 29 October 709.`,context:`The king is Itzamnaaj Bahlam III and the kneeling woman his wife, Lady K’abal Xook. The lintel was set over her door.`,source:`Mesoweb, Monuments of Yaxchilan: Lintel 24 (Sánchez Gamboa and Krempel), transcription and translation; The British Museum, object Am1923,Maud.4 (Wayback copy)`,url:`https://www.mesoweb.org/monuments/Yaxchilan/media/Lintel_24.pdf`},about:`Lady K’abal Xook kneels and draws a rope set with thorns through her tongue; the blood falls on paper in a basket, to be burned so that the smoke reaches the gods and ancestors. Her husband stands over her with a burning spear, and the rite is dated in the text to a night in October 709. The stone kept traces of red and of Maya blue, a dye of indigo fixed on clay. Alfred Maudslay had it cut from the doorway in 1882 and shipped to London; the carving is about 1.1 m high.`,learnMore:{label:`The British Museum`,url:`https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Am1923-Maud-4`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:78,heightCm:109},licence:`cc-by-sa-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Michel wal, CC BY-SA 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaxchilan_Lintel_24.jpg`,ratio:.7364},{id:`dresden-codex-page-74`,kind:`painting`,title:`Dresden Codex, page 74`,artist:`Unknown Maya scribes`,culture:`Maya, Yucatán`,date:`c. 1200–1345`,year:1200,medium:`Paint on bark paper`,place:`Sächsische Landesbibliothek (SLUB), Dresden`,caption:`A tall page of Maya glyphs and figures in black, red and blue: a sky creature above pouring water, an old goddess with a vessel, and a dark god below.`,line:{text:`Johann Christian Götze, director of the royal library in Dresden, bought it from a private owner in Vienna in 1739.`,context:`No one in Europe could read a line of it until the 1880s, when Ernst Förstemann worked out its numbers and calendar.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Dresden Codex" (Götze, Vienna, 1739; Förstemann’s facsimile 1880 and decipherment of the calendar; 78 pages, about 20 × 10 cm, 3.7 m unfolded; water damage in 1945); Wikimedia Commons description of the SLUB poster (page 74 as the flood)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dresden_Codex`},about:`The codex is one of only four Maya books to survive the Spanish conquest. It is a strip of bark paper 3.7 m long, folded into 78 pages about 20 cm high, and it holds tables that track Venus and predict eclipses with great accuracy. Page 74, shown here, is traditionally read as a flood or the destruction of the world: a creature pours water from the sky, the old goddess empties a jar, and a black god crouches below. The book was kept in a flooded cellar during the bombing of Dresden in February 1945, and some pages were badly damaged.`,learnMore:{label:`SLUB Dresden`,url:`https://digital.slub-dresden.de/werkansicht/dlf/2967/1`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`SLUB Dresden, Mscr.Dresd.R.310, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dresden_p_74_large.jpg`,ratio:.4445}],l=[{id:`leang-tedongnge-warty-pig`,kind:`painting`,title:`Warty pig, Leang Tedongnge`,artist:`Unknown painter`,culture:`Pleistocene, South Sulawesi, Indonesia`,date:`at least 45,500 years ago`,year:-43500,medium:`Red ochre on limestone`,place:`Leang Tedongnge cave, Maros-Pangkep karst, South Sulawesi`,caption:`A faded red outline of a pig with a crest of hair and facial warts on a pale cave wall, with two hand stencils above its back.`,line:{text:`A mineral crust on the pig’s rear foot is at least 45,500 years old, so the painting beneath it is older still.`,context:`The oldest dated picture of an animal. Basran Burhan found it in 2017 on a survey with Indonesian archaeologists.`,source:`Griffith University news release (dated by uranium-series on a cave popcorn deposit on the rear foot; at least 45,500 years; red ochre; survey led by Basran Burhan); Al Jazeera, 14 January 2021 (136 by 54 cm; found 2017; two handprints above the hindquarters; faces two other pigs); Brumm et al., Science Advances, January 2021`,url:`https://news.griffith.edu.au/?p=74874`},about:`The pig is a Sulawesi warty pig, a species still hunted on the island, with the crest of upright hair and the warts in front of the eyes of an adult male. It is 136 cm long and faces two other pigs that have mostly flaked away, so the panel may show a scene rather than a single animal; two hand stencils sit above its back. Calcite that grew over the paint was dated by the decay of uranium, which gives only a minimum age. The valley is reached through a cave passage and only in the dry season, and no road leads to it.`,learnMore:{label:`Griffith University`,url:`https://news.griffith.edu.au/?p=74874`},licence:`cc-by-sa-4.0`,credit:`Photo: Basran Burhan, CC BY-SA 4.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leang_Tedongnge_rock_art_panel_credit_Basran_Burhan.jpg`,ratio:1.5},{id:`lion-man-of-hohlenstein-stadel`,kind:`objects`,title:`The Lion-man of Hohlenstein-Stadel`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Aurignacian, Swabian Jura, Germany`,date:`c. 40,000 years ago`,year:-38e3,medium:`Mammoth ivory`,place:`Museum Ulm, Germany`,caption:`A standing figure carved in ivory with a lion’s head and a human body, about the length of a forearm, seen in profile.`,line:{text:`Found on 25 August 1939, a week before the war began, in more than 200 pieces. Carving it would have taken about 370 hours.`,context:`The figure is 31 cm high and about 40,000 years old, the oldest known sculpture of something that does not exist.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Lion-man of Hohlenstein-Stadel" (Otto Völzing, 25 August 1939, a week before the Second World War; radiocarbon 35,000–41,000 years; 31.1 cm; mammoth ivory; more than 200 fragments assembled from 1969 and in 1982 and 2012–13; experimental replication about 370 hours)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion-man_of_Hohlenstein-Stadel`},about:`The fragments lay in boxes through the war and for years after, and the figure was only put together in 1969; new pieces found in the cave in 2009 were added in a restoration that finished in 2013. It was cut from a mammoth tusk with flint tools, and the time it took tells us that someone in an ice-age camp was freed from hunting long enough to make it. Nothing like a lion-headed person could have been seen, so it is the earliest clear evidence of an imagined being. It is in the museum in Ulm, near the cave.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion-man_of_Hohlenstein-Stadel`},licence:`cc-by-sa-4.0`,credit:`Photo: Thilo Parg, CC BY-SA 4.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loewenmensch2.jpg`,ratio:.5626},{id:`chauvet-panel-of-the-horses`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Panel of the Horses, Chauvet Cave (replica, Grotte Chauvet 2)`,artist:`Unknown artists`,culture:`Aurignacian, Ardèche, France`,date:`c. 36,000–30,000 years ago`,year:-34e3,medium:`Charcoal on scraped limestone`,place:`Chauvet Cave, Vallon-Pont-d’Arc, France (closed; this is the Grotte Chauvet 2 copy)`,caption:`Four horse heads drawn in charcoal one above another, shaded with the fingers, beside rhinoceroses and aurochs on a pale wall.`,line:{text:`Radiocarbon dates put most of the drawings about 32,000 to 30,000 years ago. The wall was scraped clean before the first line was drawn.`,context:`Three cavers found the cave on 18 December 1994. It has never been opened to the public.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Chauvet Cave" (found 18 December 1994 by Jean-Marie Chauvet, Éliette Brunel and Christian Hillaire; radiocarbon places most painting about 32,000–30,000 years ago, two occupation phases 37,000–33,500 and 31,000–28,000 years ago; walls scraped clear before painting; replica opened 25 April 2015)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chauvet_Cave`},about:`The horses are the best known drawing in a cave that holds more than 400 animals, many of them lions, mammoths and rhinoceroses rather than the game animals usual in later caves. The artists scraped the wall white, drew in charcoal, and spread the black with their hands to model the muzzles, and in places cut lines into the rock to add relief. A rockfall sealed the entrance about 21,000 years ago, which is why the drawings survived untouched. The picture shows the copy at Grotte Chauvet 2, opened in 2015, since the cave itself is kept closed to protect it.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chauvet_Cave`},licence:`cc-by-sa-4.0`,credit:`Photo of Grotte Chauvet 2: Patrick Aventurier for Grotte Chauvet 2, CC BY-SA 4.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panneau_des_chevaux-detail%C2%A9_Patrick_Aventurier_-_Grotte_Chauvet_2_Ard%C3%A8che.jpg`,ratio:1.5},{id:`lascaux-hall-of-the-bulls`,kind:`painting`,title:`The Hall of the Bulls, Lascaux (replica, Lascaux II)`,artist:`Unknown painters`,culture:`Upper Palaeolithic, Dordogne, France`,date:`c. 17,000 years ago`,year:-15e3,medium:`Mineral pigments on limestone`,place:`Lascaux, Montignac, France (the original cave is closed; this is the Lascaux II copy)`,caption:`A cave wall crowded with painted animals: black outlined bulls, red and brown horses and deer, under an uneven rock ceiling.`,line:{text:`One of the bulls is 5.2 m long, the largest animal yet found in cave art. Four teenagers found the cave in September 1940.`,context:`Marcel Ravidat, aged 18, found the opening on 12 September 1940 after his dog went into a hole left by a fallen tree.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Lascaux" (discovery 12 September 1940 by Marcel Ravidat and friends; 36 animals in the Hall of the Bulls, one bull 5.2 m long, the largest animal discovered so far in cave art; age usually estimated at 17,000 to 22,000 years; closed since 1963; Lascaux II opened 1983)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lascaux`},about:`The Hall of the Bulls holds 36 animals, with four black aurochs bulls over the rest, painted in iron and manganese pigments that were brushed, dabbed and sometimes sprayed through a tube. Visitors’ breath and the lights began to destroy the paintings, and the cave was shut in 1963; the picture shows Lascaux II, an exact copy of the hall opened 200 m away in 1983, and a full copy of the whole cave, Lascaux IV, opened in 2016. Henri Breuil came nine days after the discovery and made the drawings that still record paintings since faded. The dating of the cave has moved over the years and now runs from about 17,000 to 22,000 years ago.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lascaux`},licence:`cc-by-sa-4.0`,credit:`Photo of Lascaux II: Elke Wetzig, CC BY-SA 4.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lascaux_II,_Salle_des_taureaux-4971.jpg`,ratio:1.875},{id:`altamira-bison`,kind:`painting`,title:`Bison from the ceiling of the polychromes, Altamira (replica, Neocueva)`,artist:`Unknown painters`,culture:`Magdalenian, Cantabria, Spain`,date:`c. 16,500–14,000 years ago`,year:-14500,medium:`Ochre and charcoal on limestone`,place:`Cave of Altamira, Santillana del Mar, Spain (closed; this is the Neocueva copy)`,caption:`A red and black bison painted over a bulge of the cave ceiling, its head lowered, among other bison.`,line:{text:`In 1879 eight-year-old María Sanz de Sautuola looked up and saw the bison on the ceiling. Experts rejected them as fakes until 1902.`,context:`Her father, Marcelino, was accused of forgery. Émile Cartailhac admitted his error in print 14 years after Marcelino died.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Cave of Altamira" (found 1868 by Modesto Cubillas; 1879 María, aged eight, and Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola; rejection led by Mortillet and Cartailhac; Cartailhac’s “Mea culpa d’un sceptique”, 1902; polychromes from the Magdalenian occupation, about 16,590–14,000 years ago; closed since 2002; replica completed 2001)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Altamira`},about:`The painters used the bulges of the low ceiling for the bodies of the bison, so that each animal stands out from the rock in the lamplight. They worked in charcoal and in ochre or hematite thinned to different strengths, and the herd of steppe bison is joined by two horses, a doe and perhaps a boar. The scholars of 1880 could not believe that ice-age hunters had painted like this, and the acceptance of Altamira in 1902 changed what people thought early humans were. The cave was closed in 2002 after mould appeared, and the picture shows the full-size copy beside the museum.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_of_Altamira`},licence:`cc-by-4.0`,credit:`Photo of the Neocueva de Altamira: Jl FilpoC, CC BY 4.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bisonte_en_el_Techo_de_Policromos,_Neocueva_de_Altamira.jpg`,ratio:1.5002},{id:`bhimbetka-boar`,kind:`painting`,title:`Horned boar, Rock Shelter 15, Bhimbetka`,artist:`Unknown painters`,culture:`Mesolithic and later, Madhya Pradesh, India`,date:`c. 10,000 years ago and later`,year:-8e3,medium:`Mineral pigments on sandstone`,place:`Bhimbetka rock shelters, Raisen district, Madhya Pradesh`,caption:`A large boar-like animal with horns painted in red on the overhang of a sandstone shelter, with smaller figures near it.`,line:{text:`The site has over 750 rock shelters, about 400 of them painted. V. S. Wakankar reported them in 1957 after seeing the rocks from a train.`,context:`Some paintings are about 10,000 years old; the shelters were lived in long before that.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Bhimbetka rock shelters" (over 750 shelters, about 400 with paintings; Wakankar reported them in 1957, having noticed rock formations like those he had seen in Spain and France; oldest paintings dated to about 10,000 BCE; UNESCO 2003). The train detail is Wakankar’s own account, repeated in Indian sources but not in the Wikipedia text read; marked partly verified.`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhimbetka_rock_shelters`},about:`Bhimbetka is a line of sandstone cliffs and boulders at the edge of the Vindhya hills, and people sheltered under them from the Stone Age into historical times. The paintings are in layers: the oldest are large animals in red and green, later ones show hunters, dancers, riders and battles, and the last are of the medieval period. The pigments were ochres, manganese and charcoal, and they survived because the overhangs kept off the rain. The shelters were listed by UNESCO in 2003.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhimbetka_rock_shelters`},licence:`cc-by-sa-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_Shelter_15,_Bhimbetka_02.jpg`,ratio:1.5},{id:`cueva-de-las-manos`,kind:`painting`,title:`Cueva de las Manos`,artist:`Unknown painters`,culture:`Hunter-gatherers of Patagonia, Santa Cruz, Argentina`,date:`c. 7300 BCE to 700 CE`,year:-7300,medium:`Mineral pigments sprayed on rock`,place:`Río Pinturas canyon, Santa Cruz, Argentina`,caption:`A rock wall covered in overlapping stencilled hands in red, white, black and ochre, made by spraying paint around a hand pressed to the stone.`,line:{text:`Of the stencilled hands, 829 are left hands and 31 right: the painter held the pipe that sprayed the paint in the right hand.`,context:`The paint was blown through bone pipes, which were found in the cave.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Cueva de las Manos" (several waves between 7,300 BC and AD 700; bone pipes for spraying paint; over 2,000 handprints; 829 left hands to 31 right; pigments of iron oxides, kaolin, natrojarosite, manganese oxide; UNESCO 1999)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cueva_de_las_Manos`},about:`The cave stands above a river canyon in Patagonia, and its walls hold more than 2,000 hands along with guanacos, hunting scenes and patterns. Each hand was made by pressing the palm to the rock and blowing pigment around it, so what remains is the outline, and most are the size of the hands of young people. The reds are iron oxide, the white is kaolin, the yellow natrojarosite and the black manganese, and gypsum helped the paint stick to the stone. The people who made the oldest hands were among the first to live this far south, and the site was listed by UNESCO in 1999.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cueva_de_las_Manos`},licence:`cc-by-sa-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Maxima20, CC BY-SA 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cueva_de_las_Manos-Santa_Cruz-Argentina.JPG`,ratio:1.3333},{id:`mask-of-agamemnon`,kind:`objects`,title:`Gold funeral mask, the so-called Mask of Agamemnon`,artist:`Unknown maker`,culture:`Mycenaean, Grave Circle A, Mycenae`,date:`c. 1550–1500 BCE`,year:-1550,medium:`Gold sheet, hammered and chased`,place:`National Archaeological Museum, Athens`,caption:`A gold face with closed eyes, a beard and moustache, and ears cut free from the sheet, beaten from a single plate.`,line:{text:`Heinrich Schliemann found it in 1876 and took it for the face of Agamemnon. It is some three centuries older than any Trojan War.`,context:`Grave V of Grave Circle A at Mycenae. The telegram “I have gazed upon the face of Agamemnon” is probably invented.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Mask of Agamemnon" (Schliemann, 1876, Grave V, Grave Circle A; dated to the 16th century BC, 300–400 years before the Trojan War; single thick gold sheet hammered over wood, details chased; the telegram “is supposed to” have been sent)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mask_of_Agamemnon`},about:`The mask was made from one thick sheet of gold, heated and hammered against a wooden form, with the beard and brows cut in afterwards with a sharp tool. It lay over the face of a man buried in a shaft grave with weapons and other gold, one of several masks from the circle of royal graves inside the citadel. Schliemann had excavated Troy a few years before and read Homer as history, so he named the finds for the king of the Greeks in the Iliad. The National Archaeological Museum in Athens lists it as number 624.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mask_of_Agamemnon`},licence:`cc-by-sa-4.0`,credit:`Photo: Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funeral_mask_Mycenae_%22Agamemnon%22,_NAMA_624,_225545.jpg`,ratio:.7502},{id:`knossos-bull-leaping-fresco`,kind:`painting`,title:`Bull-leaping fresco from Knossos`,artist:`Unknown painters`,culture:`Minoan, Crete`,date:`c. 1450 BCE`,year:-1450,medium:`Fresco on plaster, restored`,place:`Heraklion Archaeological Museum, Crete`,caption:`A restored wall painting of a charging bull, a dark-skinned figure somersaulting over its back and two pale figures at its head and tail, in a patterned border.`,line:{text:`The painting fell from an upper floor of the palace in a destruction, probably an earthquake, and was pieced together from flakes.`,context:`The darker patches are original; the rest is restoration under Arthur Evans, who dug Knossos from 1900.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Bull-Leaping Fresco" (panels from the upper storey of the east wing fell in a destruction, probably an earthquake, in Late Minoan II; the most completely restored of several stucco panels; 78.2 × 104.5 cm; Evans as director of excavation)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bull-Leaping_Fresco`},about:`Minoan painters followed the Egyptian rule of showing women with pale skin and men with dark, so the two figures holding the bull are usually read as women and the one somersaulting over its back as a man. Whether anyone really vaulted a charging bull, or whether the picture is a rite or a story, is still argued. The fresco is 78 by 105 cm, and more fragments survive than the famous reconstruction uses. It is in the museum at Heraklion with the other finds from the palace.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bull-Leaping_Fresco`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:104.5,heightCm:78.2},licence:`cc0`,credit:`Photo: Jebulon, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_leaping_minoan_fresco_archmus_Heraklion.jpg`,ratio:1.7845},{id:`menna-fishing-and-fowling`,kind:`painting`,title:`Menna and Family Hunting in the Marshes, Tomb of Menna (facsimile)`,artist:`Nina de Garis Davies`,culture:`Egyptian, Thebes; New Kingdom original`,date:`1924; original c. 1396–1347 BCE`,year:-1396,medium:`Tempera on paper`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A man stands in two papyrus boats, spearing fish on the right and throwing a stick at birds on the left, with his family and a thicket full of birds.`,line:{text:`Nina de Garis Davies copied the wall at full size in 1924. The original is painted plaster on the north wall of Menna’s chapel at Thebes.`,context:`Her copies record the damage too, including the gouged faces of Menna and his wife.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Heilbrunn Timeline entry for 30.4.48 (Wayback copy of 6 January 2020: copied from the north wall; scale 1:1; date 1924 from the Met API record); Wikipedia, "TT69" (faces of Menna and Henuttawy targeted); Wikipedia, "Nina de Garis Davies" (copies include damage)`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/548437`},about:`Menna oversaw the fields of the temple of Amun and of the king, and his tomb chapel at Sheikh Abd el-Qurna is one of the finest painted tombs of the 18th Dynasty. He is shown twice, once with a throwstick and decoy birds, once spearing two tilapia, with his wife and children crowded into the boats; a cat climbs a papyrus stalk towards a nest and a crocodile waits below. The Met explains the marsh as the untamed edge of the world, so that by hunting there Menna keeps order on behalf of the gods, and the fish, lotuses and birds all carry meanings of rebirth. The Met sent painters to Egypt from 1907 to make copies like this, about 350 in all, and the facsimile is 101 by 189 cm.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/548437`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:189,heightCm:101},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/548437`,ratio:1.8319},{id:`nefertiti-bust`,kind:`objects`,title:`Bust of Nefertiti`,artist:`Workshop of Thutmose`,culture:`Egyptian, Amarna, 18th Dynasty`,date:`c. 1351–1334 BCE`,year:-1345,medium:`Limestone, painted stucco, quartz, wax`,place:`Neues Museum, Berlin`,caption:`A painted bust of a queen with a tall flat-topped blue crown, a long neck and a broad collar, the left eye blank.`,line:{text:`Description is useless, must be seen.`,by:`Ludwig Borchardt`,context:`in his excavation diary on 6 December 1912, the day it was found in the sculptor Thutmose’s workshop at Amarna.`,source:`Staatliche Museen zu Berlin, Ägyptisches Museum, “The bust” page (Borchardt’s diary entry of 6 December 1912 in the museum’s English rendering; ÄM 23100; 49 × 24.5 × 35 cm; limestone, painted stucco, quartz, wax; c. 1351–1334 BC; donated by James Simon)`,url:`https://www.smb.museum/en/museums-institutions/aegyptisches-museum-und-papyrussammlung/collection-research/bust-of-nefertiti/the-bust/`},about:`Nefertiti was the wife of Akhenaten, the king who moved Egypt’s capital to Amarna and its worship to the sun disc. The bust is a limestone core finished in painted stucco, and it is probably a sculptor’s model: the right eye is inlaid with quartz and the left was never filled, which Borchardt only later concluded had always been so. It was found with other heads in the house of the sculptor Thutmose, whose name was on an ivory horse blinker in the ruins. The bust is 49 cm high and went to Berlin in 1913 in the division of finds, a decision Egypt has disputed ever since.`,learnMore:{label:`Staatliche Museen zu Berlin`,url:`https://www.smb.museum/en/museums-institutions/aegyptisches-museum-und-papyrussammlung/collection-research/bust-of-nefertiti/the-bust/`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:24.5,heightCm:49},licence:`cc-by-2.0`,credit:`Photo: Giovanni, Florence, CC BY 2.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nefertiti_(Nofretete_in_Berlin).jpg`,ratio:.6692},{id:`houmuwu-ding`,kind:`objects`,title:`Houmuwu ding`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Late Shang dynasty, Anyang`,date:`c. 1200 BCE`,year:-1200,medium:`Cast bronze`,place:`National Museum of China, Beijing`,caption:`A massive rectangular bronze cauldron on four thick legs with two upright handles, its sides bordered with bands of animal masks.`,line:{text:`It weighs 832.84 kg, the heaviest ancient bronze found in China. Dug up at Anyang in 1939, it is inscribed to a queen, Mother Wu.`,context:`Made for the ancestral offerings of Fu Jing, a wife of King Wu Ding, probably by her son.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Houmuwu ding" (832.84 kg; 133 cm high, 110 wide, 79 deep; unearthed 1939 at Wuguan village, Anyang; inscription Hou Mu Wu, Queen Mother Wu, temple name of Fu Jing; made after her death presumably by her son Zu Geng); Wikimedia Commons file description (heaviest bronze found in China)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houmuwu_ding`},about:`A ding is a cooking vessel, and in the Shang court bronze dings held the meat offered to the ancestors. This one is 133 cm high and was cast in piece moulds: clay sections were fitted around a core, molten bronze filled the gap, and the handles were cast and joined afterwards; the body and legs are thought to have been poured as one, which needed a great deal of metal at once. The three characters inside were read for decades as Si Mu Wu, and the museum renamed the vessel Houmuwu in 2011 after the first character was reread. It was dug up in 1939, during the Japanese occupation, and confirmed as Shang in 1946.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houmuwu_ding`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:110,heightCm:133},licence:`cc-by-sa-3.0`,credit:`Photo: Mlogic, CC BY-SA 3.0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HouMuWuDingFullView.jpg`,ratio:.7682},{id:`sanxingdui-bronze-mask`,kind:`objects`,title:`Bronze mask from Sanxingdui`,artist:`Unknown makers`,culture:`Sanxingdui culture, Sichuan`,date:`c. 1200–1100 BCE`,year:-1200,medium:`Cast bronze`,place:`Excavated at Sanxingdui, Guanghan, Sichuan; photographed on loan at the National Museum of China, Beijing`,caption:`A wide bronze face with huge almond eyes, a straight nose, a thin mouth and square ears that stick out like wings.`,line:{text:`Found in 1986 in a pit at Sanxingdui among hundreds of bronzes that had been broken, burned and buried on purpose.`,context:`The culture was unknown until a farmer dug up jades in 1927, and nothing in Chinese records names it.`,source:`Wikipedia, "Sanxingdui" (jades found 1927; sacrificial pits found 1986; objects broken, burned and carefully buried; no written records; radiocarbon dates in the 12th–11th centuries BC); Wikimedia Commons file description (Sanxingdui culture, Sichuan, 1986; National Museum exhibition)`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanxingdui`},about:`Sanxingdui was a walled city on the Chengdu plain, a thousand kilometres from the Shang capital, and its bronze faces look like nothing else from ancient China. The two pits found in 1986 held masks, heads, a bronze tree and a standing figure as tall as a man, along with elephant tusks and gold, all smashed and scorched before burial, perhaps as an offering. Some masks have pupils that stick out like stalks; this one has the same long eyes and winged ears without them. Why the city was abandoned, and what the masks were for, is not known.`,learnMore:{label:`Wikipedia`,url:`https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanxingdui`},licence:`cc0`,credit:`Photo: Gary Todd, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_Bronze_Mask,_Sanxingdui.jpg`,ratio:1.5}],u=[{id:`haeckel-discomedusae`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Discomedusae`,artist:`Ernst Haeckel`,date:`1904`,medium:`Colour lithograph by Adolf Giltsch. Plate 8 of Kunstformen der Natur`,place:`Library of Congress, Washington, D.C.`,caption:`A plate of jellyfish from Haeckel's Art Forms in Nature, centred on Desmonema annasethe and its long trailing tentacles.`,line:{text:`Haeckel named the central jellyfish Desmonema annasethe after his wife Anna Sethe, who died in 1864 after eighteen months of marriage.`,context:`She died on his 30th birthday, 16 February 1864. His text for this plate says the name keeps her memory.`,source:`Ernst Haeckel, Kunstformen der Natur (Leipzig and Vienna, 1899–1904), text sheet for plate 8, fig. 1 (Smithsonian Libraries copy on archive.org); date and length of marriage from Maya Wei-Haas, Smithsonian Magazine, 2017`,url:`https://archive.org/details/KunstformenderN00Haec`},about:`The plate comes from Kunstformen der Natur (Art Forms in Nature), published in parts from 1899 to 1904. Haeckel made the drawing and the lithographer Adolf Giltsch turned it into this colour print. Haeckel's own text says the large jellyfish in the middle is shown at life size. He was a zoologist who did much to spread Darwin's ideas in Germany, published a two-volume monograph on jellyfish, and named his house in Jena Villa Medusa.`,learnMore:{label:`Public Domain Review`,url:`https://publicdomainreview.org/collection/ernst-haeckels-jellyfish/`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Discomedusae._-_Schweibenquallen_LCCN2014645034.jpg`,ratio:.728},{id:`audubon-carolina-parrot`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Carolina Parrot`,artist:`John James Audubon`,date:`1827`,medium:`Hand-coloured engraving and aquatint by Robert Havell Jr.`,place:`National Gallery of Art, Washington`,caption:`Seven Carolina parakeets cluster on a cocklebur plant in plate 26 of Audubon's The Birds of America.`,line:{text:`The last Carolina parakeet, a captive bird named Incas, died in the Cincinnati Zoo on 21 February 1918.`,context:`The species was declared extinct in 1939. It was the only parrot native to the eastern United States.`,source:`Sarah Holzmann, "The Last Member of This Bird Species Died in Captivity on This Day in 1918", Smithsonian Magazine, 21 February 2025`,url:`https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/the-last-member-of-this-bird-species-died-in-captivity-on-this-day-in-1918-180986054/`},about:`Seven Carolina parakeets feed on a cocklebur plant in plate 26 of The Birds of America. The birds are shown at life size, as in every plate of the book, and Robert Havell made the print in engraving and aquatint before it was coloured by hand. Subscribers received the plates in sets of five over thirteen years, 435 in all, and fewer than two hundred full sets were made. Forest clearing, the feather trade and farmers who shot the birds as pests all played a part in the species' decline, but the full cause is still not understood.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery of Art`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/32167-carolina-parrot`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:60.2,heightCm:84.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`National Gallery of Art, Washington, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/32167-carolina-parrot`,ratio:.7122},{id:`merian-caterpillars-butterflies-flower`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Caterpillars, Butterflies and Flower`,artist:`Maria Sibylla Merian`,date:`1705 (1705–1771)`,medium:`Hand-colored etching and engraving`,place:`Minneapolis Institute of Art`,caption:`Two moths, their caterpillars and a flowering palisade tree, from Merian's book on the insects of Suriname.`,line:{text:`The caterpillars are not the young of the moth beside them, as Merian thought. They belong to another species.`,context:`From her 1705 book on the insects of Suriname, where she studied each insect on the plant it fed on.`,source:`Minneapolis Institute of Art, object entry for P.18,717`,url:`https://collections.artsmia.org/art/7368`},about:`In 1699 Merian sailed with her daughter Dorothea to Suriname, then a Dutch colony, and spent two years studying its plants and insects at her own expense. She published the results in 1705 as Metamorphosis insectorum Surinamensium, the first book on the natural history of Suriname. This plate shows two moths with their supposed caterpillars on a palisade tree, a plant of tropical South America that can grow twenty metres tall. Merian drew insects through their metamorphosis, on the plants where they lived and fed.`,learnMore:{label:`Minneapolis Institute of Art`,url:`https://collections.artsmia.org/art/7368`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:28.3,heightCm:38.1},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Minneapolis Institute of Art, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maria_Merian_Metamorphosis_Insectorum_Surinamensium_MIA_P18717.jpg`,ratio:.6917},{id:`redoute-fritillaria-imperialis`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Fritillaria imperialis, from Les Liliacées`,artist:`Pierre-Joseph Redouté`,date:`1802–16`,medium:`Hand-colored stipple engraving`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`A crown imperial at life size, its orange bells hanging beneath a tuft of leaves on a tall stem.`,line:{text:`Every one of the 503 plates of Les Liliacées, issued in eighty parts from 1802 to 1816, shows its flower at life size.`,context:`From Les Liliacées, made under the patronage of Josephine Bonaparte.`,source:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, object entry for 2019.282.43`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/828288`},about:`Les Liliacées is Redouté's largest work. Each plate was printed from a stipple engraving and finished in watercolour. He made the book under the patronage of Josephine Bonaparte, who grew a great variety of flowers at Malmaison; her roses became the models for his next book, Les Roses. Born in 1759 in Saint-Hubert, now in Belgium, Redouté became the best-known botanical illustrator in France.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/828288`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:35.2,heightCm:52.4},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/828288`,ratio:.6794},{id:`hooke-micrographia-flea`,kind:`illustration`,title:`A flea (Schem. XXXIV), from Micrographia`,artist:`Robert Hooke`,date:`1665`,medium:`Engraving, folding plate`,place:`Wellcome Collection, London`,caption:`A flea as seen through a microscope, engraved at large size on a fold-out plate in Robert Hooke's Micrographia.`,line:{text:`The most ingenious book that ever I read in my life.`,by:`Samuel Pepys`,context:`in his diary, 21 January 1665, of Hooke’s Micrographia, having sat up till two in the morning with it. He had ordered a copy at his bookseller on 2 January.`,source:`The Diary of Samuel Pepys, entry for Saturday 21 January 1664/65 (pepysdiary.com, from the Wheatley edition). The sentence begins "Before I went to bed I sat up till two o'clock..."`,url:`https://www.pepysdiary.com/diary/1665/01/21/`},about:`Micrographia was the first English book devoted entirely to what could be seen through a microscope, printed by order of the Royal Society. The flea is the largest plate and folds out of the book, almost half a metre long and over 35 cm tall. Each plate is based on Hooke's own drawing; as a boy he had studied with the painter Peter Lely. Hooke used the microscope to compare human work with nature: a needle point or razor edge looked rough and misshapen, while living things looked perfectly formed.`,learnMore:{label:`Wellcome Collection`,url:`https://wellcomecollection.org/works/apmaw56u`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Wellcome Collection, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://wellcomecollection.org/works/apmaw56u`,ratio:1.3365},{id:`zain-al-din-indian-roller`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Indian Roller on Sandalwood Branch`,artist:`Sheikh Zain al-Din`,culture:`Company painting, Calcutta, India`,date:`1779`,medium:`Opaque colors and ink on paper`,place:`Minneapolis Institute of Art`,caption:`An Indian roller on a sandalwood branch, head bent into its feathers, painted in Calcutta for Lady Mary Impey's natural history album.`,line:{text:`Painted in Calcutta for Lady Impey’s album of the animals and plants of her garden, which ran to 326 pictures by three artists.`,context:`Mary Impey, wife of the chief justice of Bengal, commissioned the album between 1777 and 1783.`,source:`Minneapolis Institute of Art, object entry for 2018.53.9; number of paintings from The Metropolitan Museum of Art, entry for another Impey Album painting by Zain al-Din (2012.7)`,url:`https://collections.artsmia.org/art/118880`},about:`An Indian roller rests on a sandalwood branch with its head bent into its feathers. Sheikh Zain al-Din, a Muslim trained in Mughal painting, was the foremost of the three artists Lady Impey employed, the others being Bhawani Das and Ram Das, both Hindus; he is believed to have painted from life. Leaving out the ground and the landscape, as here, comes from European natural history prints. Works like this are called Company painting: Indian artists adapting their skills to British taste under East India Company rule.`,learnMore:{label:`Minneapolis Institute of Art`,url:`https://collections.artsmia.org/art/118880`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Minneapolis Institute of Art, public domain`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Zain_ud-Din_-_Indian_Roller_on_Sandalwood.jpg`,ratio:1.3903},{id:`atkins-rhodomenia-polycarpa`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Rhodomenia Polycarpa`,artist:`Anna Atkins`,date:`c. 1853`,medium:`Cyanotype`,place:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York`,caption:`Seaweed printed white on blue by sunlight, a page from Anna Atkins's Photographs of British Algae.`,line:{text:`…to avail myself of Sir John Herschel’s beautiful process of Cyanotype, to obtain impressions of the plants themselves.`,by:`Anna Atkins`,context:`in the preface to the first part of Photographs of British Algae, 1843. Small plants were too hard to draw accurately, she wrote, so she printed them.`,source:`Anna Atkins, Photographs of British Algae: Cyanotype Impressions, part I (1843), preface, read from the Science Museum Group's scan of the page (object 1937-403/3). Full sentence: "The difficulty of making accurate drawings of objects so minute as many of the Algæ and Confervæ, has induced me to avail myself of Sir John Herschel's beautiful process of Cyanotype, to obtain impressions of the plants themselves, which I have much pleasure in offering to my botanical friends."`,url:`https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co8465394/introduction-page-from-photographs-of-british-algae-cyanotype-impressions`},about:`Photographs of British Algae was the first book to be illustrated with photographs. Atkins issued it in parts from 1843 and made each plate by laying wet seaweed on paper coated with light-sensitive iron salts and exposing it to sunlight. Herschel had invented the cyanotype process in 1842; architects later used it to copy drawings as blueprints. She took the names from William Harvey's unillustrated Manual of British Algae and wrote each label by hand, as at the foot of this page.`,learnMore:{label:`The Met`,url:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/286896`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:20,heightCm:25.3},licence:`cc0`,credit:`The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/286896`,ratio:.763},{id:`haeckel-diatomea`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Diatomea`,artist:`Ernst Haeckel`,date:`1899–1904`,medium:`Lithograph by Adolf Giltsch. Plate 4 of Kunstformen der Natur`,place:`Kunstformen der Natur, Leipzig and Vienna, 1904`,caption:`Twenty-two figures of diatoms, single-celled algae with shells of silica, drawn far larger than life on one plate of Haeckel's Art Forms in Nature.`,line:{text:`Each diatom is a single cell. Its shell of silica is made in two halves that fit together like a box and its lid.`,context:`From Haeckel's text for this plate. The slightly larger lid overlaps the lower half with a broad band.`,source:`Ernst Haeckel, Kunstformen der Natur (Leipzig and Vienna, 1899–1904), text sheet for plate 4 (Smithsonian Libraries copy on archive.org)`,url:`https://archive.org/details/KunstformenderN00Haec`},about:`Twenty-two diatoms are drawn far larger than life on plate 4 of Kunstformen der Natur. Haeckel wrote that over 2,000 species were known, most of them very small, drifting in fresh water and the sea as part of the plankton. Their shells are pierced by fine pores and often follow a regular geometry; this plate has two-sided, three-rayed, four-rayed, five-rayed and many-rayed forms. Adolf Giltsch made the lithograph from Haeckel's drawing, and the work came out in parts of ten plates, each with a text sheet.`,learnMore:{label:`Internet Archive`,url:`https://archive.org/details/KunstformenderN00Haec`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Ernst Haeckel, Kunstformen der Natur, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haeckel_Diatomea_4.jpg`,ratio:.7047},{id:`merian-guava-tarantulas`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Branch of a Guava Tree with Tarantulas and a Hummingbird`,artist:`Maria Sibylla Merian`,date:`1705`,medium:`Hand-coloured engraving by Pieter Sluyter`,place:`Smithsonian Libraries and Archives, Washington`,caption:`Large spiders, ants and a hummingbird on a guava branch, from Merian's 1705 book on the insects of Suriname.`,line:{text:`In 1699, aged 52, Merian sold most of what she owned and sailed to Suriname with her daughter to paint its insects.`,context:`Poor health and the heat forced her back to Amsterdam in 1701. The book came out in 1705.`,source:`J. Paul Getty Museum, exhibition page for "Maria Sibylla Merian & Daughters: Women of Art and Science" (2008)`,url:`https://www.getty.edu/art/exhibitions/merian/`},about:`Merian put the spiders on a guava branch because she usually found the largest ones on guava trees. She wrote that they fed on ants, and that when ants were scarce they took small birds from their nests. Her text also describes ants that strip a tree bare in a night and ants that cross gaps by forming a living bridge. She wrote that all 60 plates were painted from life in America, cut by the best engravers and printed on the best paper; this one was engraved by Pieter Sluyter.`,learnMore:{label:`Getty`,url:`https://www.getty.edu/art/exhibitions/merian/`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`Smithsonian Libraries and Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metamorphosis_insectorum_surinamensium_(Pl._18)_BHL41398715.jpg`,ratio:.662},{id:`redoute-tradescantia-virginica`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Tradescantia virginica`,artist:`Pierre Joseph Redouté`,date:`1802/1816`,medium:`Watercolour and graphite on vellum`,place:`National Gallery of Art, Washington`,caption:`Redouté's watercolour of a spiderwort, painted on vellum as a model for his book Les Liliacées and once owned by Empress Joséphine.`,line:{text:`Each spiderwort flower lasts one day. It opens in the morning and then turns to pulp.`,context:`Redouté painted this one on vellum as the model for a plate in Les Liliacées.`,source:`University of Tennessee Extension, Tennessee Smart Yards, "Tradescantia virginiana"`,url:`https://tnyards.utk.edu/tradescantia-virginiana`},about:`This watercolour on vellum is one of Redouté's finished drawings for Les Liliacées, the models from which the plates were engraved and coloured by hand. It belonged to Empress Joséphine at Malmaison, then to her son Eugène de Beauharnais and the Dukes of Leuchtenberg; it was sold in Zurich in 1935 and in New York in 1985, and given to the National Gallery of Art in 2023. Redouté left home at 13 to earn a living as an artist and learned watercolour and engraving at the Jardin du Roi in Paris.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery of Art`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/205902-tradescantia-virginica`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:34.4,heightCm:47.8},licence:`cc0`,credit:`National Gallery of Art, Washington, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/205902-tradescantia-virginica`,ratio:.7246},{id:`ehret-haemanthus`,kind:`illustration`,title:`Haemanthus`,artist:`Johannes Jacob Haid and Johannes Elias Haid after Georg Ehret`,date:`published 1750–1773`,medium:`Hand-coloured engraving on laid paper`,place:`National Gallery of Art, Washington`,caption:`A blood lily in flower, with small lettered figures of its flower, berry and sprouting seed, plate XLIV of Trew's Plantae selectae.`,line:{text:`Trew wrote that dried berries sent to him by Philip Miller began to sprout while still wrapped in paper. Figures i to p show them.`,context:`From Trew's text for this plate in Plantae selectae, published in Nuremberg from 1750.`,source:`Christoph Jacob Trew, Plantae selectae (Nuremberg, 1750–1773), text for plate XLIV (Missouri Botanical Garden copy on archive.org): "MILLERVS praeterita aestate ... misit etiam huius stirpis aliquot baccas siccas ... quarum aliquae chartis adhuc inclusae vegetare coeperunt"`,url:`https://archive.org/details/mobot31753003431688`},about:`A blood lily in flower, with lettered figures of its parts: a is the whole flower, c the corolla cut open to show six stamens, f and g the berry whole and cut across, h the seed. Ehret painted the plants from living specimens in the gardens of London collectors; Trew collected and annotated the paintings, and Johann Jacob Haid of Augsburg engraved them in copper. Ehret, born in Heidelberg in 1708, learned to draw from his father, a market gardener, and died in London in 1770. Trew printed 100 of his drawings in Plantae selectae.`,learnMore:{label:`National Gallery of Art`,url:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/73716-haemanthus`},scale:{kind:`size`,widthCm:36.8,heightCm:52.2},licence:`cc0`,credit:`National Gallery of Art, Washington, CC0`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nga.gov/artworks/73716-haemanthus`,ratio:.6692},{id:`trouvelot-saturn`,kind:`illustration`,title:`The planet Saturn. Observed on November 30, 1874`,artist:`Étienne Léopold Trouvelot`,date:`1881–82`,medium:`Chromolithograph after a pastel drawing`,place:`New York Public Library`,caption:`Saturn as Trouvelot saw it through a telescope on 30 November 1874, plate X of his portfolio of astronomical drawings.`,line:{text:`The view is upside down, as in a refracting telescope: south is at the top and north at the bottom.`,context:`Trouvelot's manual to the plates, 1882. He drew Saturn at the Harvard refractor on 30 November 1874.`,source:`É. L. Trouvelot, The Trouvelot Astronomical Drawings Manual (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1882), introduction: "The drawings representing telescopic views are inverted, as they appear in a refracting telescope — the South being upward, the North downward, the East on the right, and the West on the left."`,url:`https://archive.org/details/trouvelotastrono00trourich`},about:`To keep the proportions right, Trouvelot put a glass reticule ruled with fine squares at the telescope's focus and copied the image square by square onto paper ruled the same way. The dark inner ring, which he called the gauze or dusky ring, had been found in 1850 with the same 15-inch Harvard refractor he used here. He drew a notch in the planet's shadow where it crosses the rings and took it as a sign that the ring surface was not flat. Trouvelot made the originals in pastel, and Charles Scribner's Sons published fifteen of them in 1881–82 as a portfolio of chromolithographs.`,learnMore:{label:`New York Public Library`,url:`https://digitalcollections.nypl.org/items/b5a2fa50-c62b-012f-2156-58d385a7bc34`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`New York Public Library, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons`,sourceUrl:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trouvelot_-_The_planet_Saturn_-_1874.jpg`,ratio:1.2783,night:!0}],d=[{id:`webb-cosmic-cliffs`,kind:`space`,title:`Cosmic Cliffs in the Carina Nebula`,artist:`James Webb Space Telescope`,date:`2022`,medium:`Near-infrared image, NIRCam, six filters`,place:`NGC 3324 in the Carina Nebula, 7,600 light-years away in Carina`,distance:`7,600 light-years`,caption:`The edge of a cavity of gas and dust in the young star-forming region NGC 3324, seen in near-infrared light.`,line:{text:`The tallest peaks in this picture are about seven light-years high.`,context:`The wall of a cavity carved out of NGC 3324 by hot young stars above the top of the frame.`,source:`NASA, “NASA’s Webb Reveals Cosmic Cliffs, Glittering Landscape of Star Birth”, 12 July 2022`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/nasas-webb-reveals-cosmic-cliffs-glittering-landscape-of-star-birth/`},about:`The ridge is the edge of a giant cavity in the star-forming region NGC 3324, carved out by ultraviolet light and winds from massive young stars above the frame. What looks like steam rising off it is hot ionised gas and dust streaming away from the nebula. Infrared light passes through the dust, so NIRCam shows hundreds of stars hidden in visible light; the youngest are red dots in the dark part of the cloud. The colours are assigned, one hue per filter, and the frame is about 16 light-years across.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/cosmic-cliffs-in-the-carina-nebula-nircam-image/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:7600,note:`The light in this picture left the nebula about 7,600 years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/cosmic-cliffs-in-the-carina-nebula-nircam-image/`,ratio:1.7271,night:!0},{id:`webb-first-deep-field`,kind:`space`,title:`Webb's First Deep Field`,artist:`James Webb Space Telescope`,date:`2022`,medium:`Near-infrared image, NIRCam, six filters`,place:`Galaxy cluster SMACS 0723 in Volans`,caption:`Thousands of galaxies in and behind the galaxy cluster SMACS 0723, in Webb's first deep image of the distant universe.`,line:{text:`The whole picture covers a patch of sky about the size of a grain of sand held at arm’s length.`,source:`NASA, “NASA’s Webb Delivers Deepest Infrared Image of Universe Yet”, 12 July 2022`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/nasas-webb-delivers-deepest-infrared-image-of-universe-yet/`},about:`The galaxy cluster SMACS 0723 appears here as it was 4.6 billion years ago. Its mass bends and magnifies the light of galaxies behind it, which show as stretched arcs; some are seen as they were when the universe was less than a billion years old. The image combines NIRCam exposures at different wavelengths totalling 12.5 hours and reaches deeper in the infrared than Hubble’s deepest fields, which took weeks. President Biden unveiled it at the White House on 11 July 2022, the day before the rest of Webb’s first images.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/nasas-webb-delivers-deepest-infrared-image-of-universe-yet/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:46e8,note:`The light from the cluster left it about 4.6 billion years ago, about when the Earth formed.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/webbs-first-deep-field-nircam-image/`,ratio:.9799,night:!0},{id:`webb-pillars-of-creation`,kind:`space`,title:`Pillars of Creation`,artist:`James Webb Space Telescope`,date:`2022`,medium:`Near-infrared image, NIRCam, six filters`,place:`Eagle Nebula (M16), 6,500 light-years away in Serpens`,distance:`6,500 light-years`,caption:`Columns of cool gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula, where new stars are forming, in Webb's near-infrared view.`,line:{text:`The red orbs with diffraction spikes are newly formed stars, only a few hundred thousand years old.`,source:`NASA, “NASA’s Webb Takes Star-Filled Portrait of Pillars of Creation”, 19 October 2022`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/nasas-webb-takes-star-filled-portrait-of-pillars-of-creation/`},about:`These are columns of cool gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula, where new stars are forming. Knots of gas inside the pillars collapse under their own gravity, heat up and begin to shine; the wavy lines at the edges are jets from stars still forming, striking the gas around them. Almost no distant galaxies show, because dust in the dense disk of the Milky Way blocks the view even in near-infrared light. The image is about 8 light-years across; Hubble first photographed these pillars in 1995 and again in 2014.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/pillars-of-creation-nircam-image/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:6500,note:`The light in this picture left the nebula about 6,500 years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI; Image Processing: Joseph DePasquale (STScI), Anton Koekemoer (STScI), Alyssa Pagan (STScI)`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/pillars-of-creation-nircam-image/`,ratio:.5773,night:!0},{id:`webb-southern-ring-nebula`,kind:`space`,title:`Southern Ring Nebula`,artist:`James Webb Space Telescope`,date:`2022`,medium:`Near-infrared image, NIRCam, six filters`,place:`NGC 3132, about 2,500 light-years away in Vela`,distance:`2,500 light-years`,caption:`Shells of gas and dust thrown off by a dying star in the planetary nebula NGC 3132, seen in near-infrared light.`,line:{text:`The bright star at the centre did not make the nebula. Its dim companion, at lower left along a diffraction spike, did.`,source:`NASA, Southern Ring Nebula (NIRCam image), image page`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/southern-ring-nebula-nircam-image/`},about:`The two stars circle each other in a tight orbit. Over thousands of years the dimmer one has thrown off at least eight layers of gas and dust, spraying material in many directions, which gives the rings their jagged shape. Hundreds of straight lines of light cross the rings: starlight from the bright star passing through holes in the gas. The nebula is seen almost face-on; from the side it would look like two bowls joined at the bottom, with a large hole at the centre, and the bright angled line at upper left is a galaxy seen edge-on.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/southern-ring-nebula-nircam-image/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:2500,note:`The light in this picture left the nebula about 2,500 years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/southern-ring-nebula-nircam-image/`,ratio:1.0738,night:!0},{id:`hubble-extreme-deep-field`,kind:`space`,title:`Hubble eXtreme Deep Field (XDF)`,artist:`Hubble Space Telescope`,date:`2012`,medium:`Visible and near-infrared image, ACS and WFC3, eight filters`,place:`A patch of sky in Fornax, at the centre of the Hubble Ultra Deep Field`,caption:`About 5,500 galaxies in a tiny patch of sky at the centre of the Hubble Ultra Deep Field.`,line:{text:`Ten years of exposures of one small patch of sky, a fraction of the Moon’s width, show about 5,500 galaxies.`,source:`NASA, Hubble eXtreme Deep Field (XDF), image page`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/hubble-extreme-deep-field-xdf/`},about:`The image combines more than 2,000 exposures of the same field, taken over ten years with the Advanced Camera for Surveys and Wide Field Camera 3, for a total exposure of 2 million seconds, about 23 days. The patch measures 2.3 by 2 arcminutes. The faintest galaxies are one ten-billionth as bright as the eye can see. The large, fuzzy red galaxies have stopped forming stars and are remnants of collisions; the tiny faint ones are young galaxies of the kind from which today’s large galaxies grew.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/hubble-extreme-deep-field-xdf/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:132e8,note:`The light from the faintest galaxies here left them about 13.2 billion years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, G. Illingworth, D. Magee, and P. Oesch (University of California, Santa Cruz), R. Bouwens (Leiden University), and the HUDF09 Team`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/hubble-extreme-deep-field-xdf/`,ratio:1.1463,night:!0},{id:`hubble-westerlund-2`,kind:`space`,title:`Westerlund 2`,artist:`Hubble Space Telescope`,date:`2015`,medium:`Visible and near-infrared image, ACS and WFC3`,place:`Gum 29, 20,000 light-years away in Carina`,distance:`20,000 light-years`,caption:`A young cluster of about 3,000 stars inside the star-forming nebula Gum 29, released for Hubble's 25th anniversary.`,line:{text:`The bright blue stars scattered across the picture are mostly in the foreground, not in the cluster.`,source:`NASA, “NASA Unveils Celestial Fireworks as Official Image for Hubble 25th Anniversary”, 23 April 2015`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/goddard/nasa-unveils-celestial-fireworks-as-official-image-for-hubble-25th-anniversary/`},about:`Westerlund 2 is a cluster of about 3,000 stars, about 2 million years old, inside the nebula Gum 29; it holds some of the hottest, brightest and most massive stars in the Milky Way. The red dots are stars still wrapped in gas and dust that have not yet ignited hydrogen in their cores. In the nebula, red shows hydrogen and blue-green mostly oxygen, and the pillars, a few light-years tall, point towards the cluster. The image was released in April 2015 for Hubble’s 25th anniversary; the cluster is named for Bengt Westerlund, who discovered it in the 1960s.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/westerlund-2/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:2e4,note:`The light in this picture left the cluster about 20,000 years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, the Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA), A. Nota (ESA/STScI), and the Westerlund 2 Science Team`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/westerlund-2/`,ratio:1.3347,night:!0},{id:`hubble-lagoon-nebula`,kind:`space`,title:`Lagoon Nebula`,artist:`Hubble Space Telescope`,date:`2018`,medium:`Visible-light image, WFC3, four filters`,place:`Lagoon Nebula (M8), 4,000 light-years away in Sagittarius`,distance:`4,000 light-years`,caption:`The heart of the Lagoon Nebula, where the young giant star Herschel 36 is blasting away the cloud it formed in.`,line:{text:`From Earth, in binoculars, the nebula is only a smudge of light with a bright core.`,source:`NASA, Lagoon Nebula (Visible-light View), image page`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/lagoon-nebula-visible-light-view/`},about:`At the centre is Herschel 36, a star 32 times the mass of the Sun, eight times hotter and nearly nine times its diameter, about a million years old. Its radiation and winds push the dust away in sheets: oxygen glows blue behind the blown-out cavity, the top of the cavity lit by the star is yellow, and the reddish areas are nitrogen. The dark trunks at the edges are dense parts of the cloud that resist erosion, where new stars are forming. The view is about 4 light-years across, taken between 12 and 18 February 2018.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/lagoon-nebula-visible-light-view/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:4e3,note:`The light in this picture left the nebula about 4,000 years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, and STScI`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/lagoon-nebula-visible-light-view/`,ratio:.8492,night:!0},{id:`hubble-crab-nebula-mosaic`,kind:`space`,title:`A Giant Hubble Mosaic of the Crab Nebula`,artist:`Hubble Space Telescope`,date:`2005`,medium:`Visible-light mosaic, WFPC2, 24 exposures`,place:`Crab Nebula (M1), 6,500 light-years away in Taurus`,distance:`6,500 light-years`,caption:`The six-light-year-wide, expanding remains of a star whose supernova explosion was recorded on Earth in 1054.`,line:{text:`I humbly observe that a guest star has appeared. Above the star in question there is a faint glow, yellow in colour.`,by:`Yang Weide`,context:`court astronomer, in a memorial to the Song emperor, 1054. The star was seen by day for 23 days; this picture shows what remains of it.`,source:`Song huiyao (Sung-hui-yao), chapter 52, English translation on the University of Colorado JILA page “Sung dynasty chronicles of the appearance of the Crab Nebula Supernova of 1054 AD” (translator not credited on the page)`,url:`https://jila.colorado.edu/~ajsh/courses/astr2030_09/mccray/sung.html`},about:`Chinese astronomers recorded the explosion in 1054 as a guest star that could be seen in daytime for 23 days; in 1928 Edwin Hubble first proposed linking the nebula to it. At the centre is a neutron star, the crushed core of the exploded star, whose beams appear to pulse 30 times a second as it rotates; electrons whirling around its magnetic field at nearly the speed of light make the bluish glow inside. The orange filaments are the remains of the star, mostly hydrogen. The mosaic joins 24 exposures taken in 1999 and 2000; the name comes from a drawing made by Lord Rosse in 1844.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/a-giant-hubble-mosaic-of-the-crab-nebula/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:6500,note:`The light in this picture left the nebula about 6,500 years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, J. Hester and A. Loll (Arizona State University)`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/a-giant-hubble-mosaic-of-the-crab-nebula/`,ratio:1,night:!0},{id:`hubble-andromeda-mosaic`,kind:`space`,title:`Hubble M31 PHAT+PHAST Mosaic`,artist:`Hubble Space Telescope`,date:`2025`,medium:`Mosaic, ACS, two filters`,place:`Andromeda Galaxy (M31), 2.5 million light-years away in Andromeda`,distance:`2.5 million light-years`,caption:`The disk of the Andromeda Galaxy in the largest photomosaic ever assembled from Hubble observations.`,line:{text:`Hubble resolves more than 200 million stars here, only those brighter than the Sun. Andromeda holds about a trillion.`,source:`NASA, “NASA’s Hubble Traces Hidden History of Andromeda Galaxy”, 16 January 2025`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-traces-hidden-history-of-andromeda-galaxy/`},about:`The mosaic joins about 600 fields of view into at least 2.5 billion pixels, from more than 1,000 Hubble orbits spread over more than a decade. The PHAT survey covered the northern half of the disk; the later PHAST survey added about 100 million stars in the southern half. The disk is tilted 77 degrees to our line of sight, so it is seen almost edge-on. A century ago Edwin Hubble showed that this “spiral nebula” lies far outside the Milky Way, at about 2.5 million light-years; until then many astronomers thought the Milky Way was the whole universe.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-traces-hidden-history-of-andromeda-galaxy/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,lightYears:25e5,note:`The light in this picture left Andromeda about 2.5 million years ago.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA, ESA, Benjamin Williams (UWashington), Zhuo Chen (UWashington), L. Clifton Johnson (Northwestern); Image Processing: Joseph DePasquale (STScI)`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/hubble-m31-phatphast-mosaic/`,ratio:4.2755,night:!0},{id:`apollo17-blue-marble`,kind:`space`,title:`The Blue Marble`,artist:`Apollo 17 crew`,date:`1972`,medium:`Colour photograph, 70 mm Hasselblad camera with 80 mm lens`,place:`Earth, seen from Apollo 17 on the way to the Moon`,distance:`About 18,000 miles`,caption:`The whole sunlit Earth, from the Mediterranean to Antarctica, photographed by the Apollo 17 crew on December 7, 1972.`,line:{text:`I’ll tell you, if there ever was a fragile-appearing piece of blue in space, it’s the Earth right now.`,by:`Harrison Schmitt`,context:`to Mission Control, 5 hours 49 minutes into the flight, 7 December 1972, on the way to the Moon.`,source:`Apollo 17 Flight Journal, Day 1: “Human Weathersat”, air-to-ground transcript at 005:49:50 GET`,url:`https://apollojournals.org/afj/ap17fj/04_day01_human_weathersat.html`},about:`The crew photographed the whole sunlit disc of Earth, from the Mediterranean to Antarctica, on 7 December 1972 on the way to the Moon, with the Sun behind the spacecraft. Almost the whole coastline of Africa is visible, with the Arabian Peninsula to the north-east and Madagascar off the coast; it was the first time an Apollo trajectory made it possible to photograph the south polar ice cap. All three crew members, Eugene Cernan, Ronald Evans and Harrison Schmitt, used the Hasselblad cameras, so it cannot be said for certain who took it. Stewart Brand had campaigned since 1966 for a photograph of the whole Earth and later put this one on the cover of his Whole Earth Catalog.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://www.nasa.gov/image-detail/amf-as17-148-22727/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,km:29e3,note:`Taken from about 29,000 km away.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nasa.gov/image-detail/amf-as17-148-22727/`,ratio:1,night:!0},{id:`apollo8-earthrise`,kind:`space`,title:`Earthrise`,artist:`Bill Anders, Apollo 8`,date:`1968`,medium:`Colour photograph, Hasselblad camera with 250 mm telephoto lens`,place:`Earth above the Moon's horizon, seen from lunar orbit`,distance:`240,000 statute miles`,caption:`Earth rising above the Moon's horizon, photographed by Bill Anders from Apollo 8 on December 24, 1968.`,line:{text:`Oh my God, look at that picture over there! There’s the Earth comin’ up. Wow, is that pretty!`,by:`Bill Anders`,context:`on the onboard recorder, 24 December 1968. Borman: “Hey don’t take that, it’s not scheduled.” Anders asked for a roll of colour film.`,source:`NASA, “The Story Behind Apollo 8’s Famous Earthrise Photo”, 21 December 2018, quoting the Apollo 8 onboard voice transcript`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-story-behind-apollo-8s-famous-earthrise-photo/`},about:`Anders photographed Earth rising above the Moon’s horizon from lunar orbit on 24 December 1968. Earthrise had happened on each of the three previous orbits, but no window faced that way; it came into view only because Frank Borman was rolling the spacecraft to a new orientation. Anders first took a black-and-white frame, then loaded colour film and took this one with a 250 mm lens; the lunar horizon is about 570 km from the spacecraft and the view about 150 km wide there, and on Earth the sunset line crosses Africa. In 2018 the International Astronomical Union named a 40 km crater in the picture “Anders’ Earthrise”.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-story-behind-apollo-8s-famous-earthrise-photo/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,km:384400,note:`Earth seen from lunar orbit, about 384,000 km away.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA`,sourceUrl:`https://www.nasa.gov/image-detail/amf-as08-14-2383/`,ratio:1,night:!0},{id:`voyager1-pale-blue-dot`,kind:`space`,title:`Pale Blue Dot Revisited`,artist:`Voyager 1`,date:`1990, reprocessed 2020`,medium:`Colour composite, Narrow-Angle Camera, violet, blue and green filters`,place:`Earth, seen from the edge of the solar system`,distance:`3.8 billion miles (6 billion kilometres)`,caption:`Earth as a bright speck inside a ray of scattered sunlight, photographed by Voyager 1 from about 6 billion kilometres away.`,line:{text:`Look again at that dot. That’s here. That’s home. That’s us.`,by:`Carl Sagan`,context:`in his book Pale Blue Dot, 1994. Sagan had asked for the photograph, which Voyager 1 took on 14 February 1990.`,source:`Carl Sagan, Pale Blue Dot (1994), as excerpted on The Planetary Society’s “Pale Blue Dot” page`,url:`https://www.planetary.org/worlds/pale-blue-dot`},about:`Earth is the speck inside one of the bands of light, filling less than a single pixel; the bands are sunlight scattered inside the camera’s optics, and one of them happens to cross Earth. Voyager 1 took the frames on 14 February 1990 as part of a 60-image family portrait of the solar system, 34 minutes before its cameras were switched off for good. It could not be taken earlier in the mission because the Sun was still close and bright enough to damage the cameras; green, blue and violet filter images were mapped to red, green and blue, so the colour is not true. JPL engineer Kevin M. Gill made this 2020 version with input from two of the original planners.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/pale-blue-dot-revisited/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,km:6e9,note:`Taken from about 6 billion km. Light from Earth took about five and a half hours to reach the camera.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA/JPL-Caltech`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/pale-blue-dot-revisited/`,ratio:1.0106,night:!0},{id:`cassini-day-the-earth-smiled`,kind:`space`,title:`The Day the Earth Smiled`,artist:`Cassini`,date:`2013`,medium:`Natural-colour mosaic, wide-angle camera, 141 images`,place:`Saturn, seen from inside its shadow`,distance:`746,000 miles (1.2 million kilometres)`,caption:`Saturn and its rings backlit by the Sun, with Earth as a pale blue dot at lower right.`,line:{text:`On 19 July 2013 NASA asked people to look up and wave at Saturn while Cassini took this picture. Earth is the blue dot at lower right.`,context:`The first time people were told in advance that Earth would be photographed from so far away.`,source:`NASA/JPL, “NASA Interplanetary Probes to Take Pictures of Earth”, 18 July 2013; NASA/JPL Photojournal PIA17172`,url:`https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-interplanetary-probes-to-take-pictures-of-earth/`},about:`Cassini had moved into Saturn’s shadow; with the planet blocking the Sun, the cameras could safely look back towards the inner solar system. Earth sits between the bright blue E ring and the faint G ring, with the Moon a small bump at its lower right; Venus is a white dot at upper left and Mars a faint red dot. Earth, Venus and Mars are brightened eight and a half times relative to Saturn so that they show. Cassini took 323 images in just over four hours; this natural-colour mosaic uses 141 wide-angle frames through red, green and blue filters and spans about 650,000 km.`,learnMore:{label:`NASA`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/the-day-the-earth-smiled/`},scale:{kind:`distance`,km:144e7,note:`Earth was 1.44 billion km away; its light took about 80 minutes to reach Cassini.`},licence:`public-domain`,credit:`NASA/JPL-Caltech/SSI`,sourceUrl:`https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/the-day-the-earth-smiled/`,ratio:2.5714,night:!0}],f=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...o,...a,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d];export{f as PICTURES};